function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:`${e.marker}) ${r}`}})}var t={id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`In the city of Athens, Georgia, there exists a rather curious local landmark—a large white oak that is almost universally stated to own itself. Because of this, it is considered one of the most famous trees in the world. So how did this tree come to own itself and the land around it?`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`Sometime in the 19th century a Georgian called Colonel William Jackson reportedly took a liking to the said tree and endeavored to protect it from any danger. As to why he loved it so, the earliest documented account of this story is an anonymously written front page article in the Athens Weekly Banner published on August 12, 1890. It states, "Col. Jackson had watched the tree grow from his childhood, and grew to love it almost as he would a human. Its luxuriant leaves and sturdy limbs had often protected him from the heavy rains, and out of its highest branches he had many a time gotten the eggs of the feathered singers. He watched its growth, and when reaching a ripe old age he saw the tree standing in its magnificent proportions, he was pained to think that after his death it would fall into the hands of those who might destroy it."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Towards this end, Jackson transferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree and a little land around it to the tree itself. The deed read, "W. H. Jackson for and in consideration of the great affection which he bears the said tree, and his great desire to see it protected has conveyed unto the said oak tree entire possession of itself and of all land within eight feet of it on all sides."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`In time, the tree came to be something of a tourist attraction, known as The Tree That Owns Itself. However, in the early 20th century, the tree started showing signs of its slow death, with little that could be done about it. Father time comes for us all eventually, even our often long lived, tall and leafy fellow custodians (看管者) of Earth. Finally, on October 9, 1942, the over 30 meter tall and 200-400 year old tree fell, rumor has it, as a result of a severe windstorm and/or via having previously died and its roots rotted.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`About four years later, members of the Junior Ladies Garden Club (who'd tended to the tree before its unfortunate death) tracked down a small tree grown from a nut taken from the original tree. And so it was that on October 9, 1946, under the direction of Professor Roy Bowden of the College of Agriculture at the University of Georgia, this little tree was transplanted to the location of its ancestor. A couple of months later, an official ceremony was held featuring none other than the Mayor of Athens, Robert L McWhorter, to commemorate the occasion.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`This new tree became known as The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself and it was assumed that, as the original tree's heir, it naturally inherited the land it stood on. Of course, there are many dozens of other trees known to exist descending from the original, as people taking a nut from it to grow elsewhere was a certainty. That said, to date, none of the original tree's other children have petitioned the courts for their share of the land, so it seems all good. In any event, The Son of the Tree That Owns Itself still stands today, though often referred to simply as The Tree That Owns Itself.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`This all brings us around to whether Jackson ever actually gave legal ownership of the tree to itself in the first place and whether such a deed is legally binding.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Well, to begin with, it turns out Jackson only spent about three years of his life in Athens, starting at the age of 43 from 1829 to 1832, sort of dismissing the idea that he loved the tree from spending time under it as a child and watching it grow, and then worrying about what would happen to it after he died. Further, an extensive search of land ownership records in Athens does not seem to indicate Jackson ever owned the land the tree sits on.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`He did live on a lot of land directly next to it for those three years, but whether he owned that land or not isn't clear. Whatever the case, in 1832 a four acre parcel, which included the land the tree was on and the neighboring land Jackson lived on, among others, was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward. In the transaction, Ward was required to pay Jackson a sum of $1,200 (about $31,000 today), either for the property itself or simply in compensation for improvements Jackson had made on the lot. In the end, whether he ever owned the neighboring lot or was simply allowed to use it while he allegedly worked at the University, he definitely never owned the lot the tree grew on, which is the most important bit for the topic at hand.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`After Professor Ward purchased the land, Jackson and his family purchased a 655 acre parcel a few miles away and moved there. Ten years later, in 1844, Jackson seemed to have come into financial difficulties and had his little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and auctioned off to settle the mortgage. Thus, had he owned some land in Athens itself, including the land the tree sat on, presumably he would have sold it to raise funds or otherwise had it taken as well.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`And whatever the case there, Jackson would have known property taxes needed to be paid on the deeded land for the tree to be truly secure in its future. Yet no account or record indicates any trust or the like was set up to facilitate this.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`On top of all this, there is no hard evidence such a deed ever existed, despite the fact that deed records in Athens go back many decades before Jackson's death in 1876 and that it was supposed to have existed in 1890 in the archives according to the original anonymous news reporter who claims to have seen it.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`As you might imagine from all of this, few give credit to this side of the story. So how did all of this come about then?`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`It is speculated to have been invented by the imagination of the said anonymous author at the Athens Weekly Banner in the aforementioned 1890 front page article titled "Deeded to Itself", which by the way contained several elements that are much more easily proved to be false. As to why the author would do this, it's speculated perhaps it was a 19th century version of a click-bait thought exercise on whether it would be legal for someone to deed such a non-conscious living thing to itself or not.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`Whatever the case, the next known instance of the Tree That Owns Itself being mentioned wasn't until 1901 in the Centennial Edition of that same paper, the Athens Weekly Banner. This featured another account very clearly just copying the original article published about a decade before, only slightly reworded. The next account was in 1906, again in the Athens Weekly Banner, again very clearly copying the original account, only slightly reworded, the 19th century equivalent of re-posts when the audience has forgotten about the original.`}]},n={id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`According to recent research, behavioral change involves physical changes in the brain. In the past decade, researchers have shown that when it comes to the duration of making a new behavior a deep-rooted habit there is not a simple answer. Even for the most productive and disciplined among us, undoing something that has become an automatic part of who we are takes more than an overnight effort. Once we've successfully made that change, we then have to make other adjustments to our lives to ensure that we continue to maintain it, which is often a whole other challenge in itself.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`At its core, success in changing and maintaining a behavior rarely occurs without the introduction of some sort of system. When there isn't the right framework in place, we face a greater likelihood of derailing our hard-earned progress. To ensure success in changing and maintaining a behavior, we should stay away from some detrimental habits.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`The first one to avoid is relying on willpower. Think about the last time you vowed to resist a temptation. Perhaps you didn't want to check your phone every 15 minutes, or you were determined not to reach for a chocolate bar at 3 p.m. Think about how difficult it must have been not to glance at your phone when it was within reach, or not to walk to the vending machine when your afternoon slump hit.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`The research on whether we have finite or infinite willpower is inconclusive, but experts do generally agree that you can't change and sustain a habit if you rely on your willpower alone. The old military saying "You never rise to the occasion, you only sink to the level of training" also applies to behavior change. The idea is simple—you repeat something so many times that it becomes automatic.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Think about what else you can change about your surroundings that makes it easier for you to perform this change on a daily basis. This is called your "cue." Basically, it's a trigger to perform that particular habit. If you don't want to reach for a sugary treat at 3 p.m., have a box of herbal tea ready at your desk. When 3 p.m. comes around, that's your cue to pour yourself a cup of hot water and drink that tea, instead of walking to the vending machine.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`The second one to avoid is focusing on negative goals. Sometimes, it's not your process that lets you down, but the habit that you want to change in the first place. For starters, not eating chocolate to beat your afternoon slump is a harder goal than swapping chocolate for herbal tea when you reach the designated time. Your brain wants to find routines that have succeeded in the past and allow you to repeat those actions again in the future without having to think about them explicitly. However, this habit-learning system isn't so effective when it comes to learning not to do something. That's why rather than giving up something, think about introducing something in its place. Focus on actions you are going to take that will ultimately conflict with the behaviors you want to stop. When your attention is on doing something new, you give your habit system a chance to operate.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`The third one to avoid is using the same strategies in different circumstances. Because we are creatures of habit, it's natural to assume that when we do manage to adopt and sustain a desirable behavior, that same strategy will work when we want to make another behavior change. But that's not always the case. Sometimes, the system that got you to change one behavior might not work for another.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`Sometimes we become accustomed to relying on our guts when it comes to decision-making. This serves us well in certain situations, but can hinder us in others especially when we need to consider metrics and data, rather than letting our instinct override everything. For example, if you want to stop checking your email first thing in the morning, you might decide to substitute another activity in its place. But if you want to stop indulging in video games, simply deciding you will go for a run might not be as effective. You might need to introduce another reinforcement, such as meeting a friend and booking an exercise class together.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`The fourth one to avoid is not forgiving ourselves for slipping up. Of course, even the best-laid plans fail sometimes. You might have stuck to your screen-free nighttime routine for five days, and then a big project landed on your desk and you found yourself in bed with your laptop before you went to sleep. Or you prepared meals on Sunday and stuck to eating healthy dinners at home, but by Friday you found yourself so exhausted and opted to order greasy takeout. Life happens and even if your behavior change is small, every single day can prove pretty inflexible, and at some point your luck may run out, even if just for a day. The perfectionist in you might be screaming to abandon your goals altogether, but try to see it in the bigger picture. Just because you might have temporarily strayed off course doesn't mean you can't start afresh the next day.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The final one to avoid is discounting small progress. There's a habit that many perfectionists tend to fall into when they try to establish a behavior change. They focus too much on the big goal and don't take the time to celebrate the small progress they make in the process. Your brain responds to rewards. The basal ganglia, the brain region linked to our performance of habits, is most active at the beginning of a behavior, when the habit is cured, and at the end, when it's rewarded. Say your goal is to run five miles three times a week, and this week you ran one mile on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday. Rather than focusing on how far you've gone toward your goal, think about how you can reward yourself for the progress you've made. It doesn't have to be big or expensive; it can be something as simple as making your favorite fruit juice after your run. Whatever your reward, it has to be more than just the activity itself to get you going.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Initiating a new behavior usually seems like the hardest part of the process of change. However, people often fail to adequately prepare for maintaining it. One of the reasons for this is because we mistakenly believe the strategies we used to initiate the change will be equally effective in helping us continue the change. But they won't. Where changing a strongly deep-rooted habit requires changing our belief about that habit that penetrates deeply into our lives, continually manifesting that wisdom requires that we maintain a positive outlook. If our mood is low, the wisdom to behave differently seems to disappear and we go back to eating more and exercising less. The key, then, to maintaining new behaviors is to be happy! Which is why it's so hard to maintain new behaviors.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Remember, overcoming the behavioral inertia that prevents us from implementing new changes, like eating a healthy diet or exercising, can benefit us in the long run and can improve our physical and mental health. No one was born with habits. They were all learned, and can all, therefore, be unlearned. The question is: how badly do you really want to change?`}]},r={id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp`,title:`长篇阅读（信息匹配）`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Phyllis Hartman knows what it's like to make one's way through the depths of office meeting hell. Managers at one of her former human resources jobs arranged so many meetings that attendees would fall asleep at the table or intentionally arrive late. With hours of her day blocked up with unnecessary meetings, she was often forced to make up her work during overtime. "I was actually working more hours than I probably would have needed to get the work done," says Hartman, who is founder and president of PGHR Consulting in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`She isn't alone in her frustration. Between 11 million and 55 million meetings are held each day in the United States, costing most organisations between 7% and 15% of their personnel budgets. Every week, employees spend about six hours in meetings, while the average manager meets for a staggering 23 hours.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`And though experts agree that traditional meetings are essential for making certain decisions and developing strategy, some employees view them as one of the most unnecessary parts of the workday. The result is not only hundreds of billions of wasted dollars, but an annoyance of what organisational psychologists call "meeting recovery syndrome (MRS)": time spent cooling off and regaining focus after a useless meeting. If you run to the office kitchen to get some relief with colleagues after a frustrating meeting, you're likely experiencing meeting recovery syndrome.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Meeting recovery syndrome is a concept that should be familiar to almost anyone who has held a formal job. It isn't ground-breaking to say workers feel fatigued after a meeting, but only in recent decades have scientists deemed the condition worthy of further investigation. With its links to organisational efficiency and employee wellbeing, MRS has attracted the attention of psychologists aware of the need to understand its precise causes and cures.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`Today, in so far as researchers can hypothesize, MRS is most easily understood as a slow renewal of finite mental and physical resources. When an employee sits through an ineffective meeting their brain power is essentially being drained away. Meetings drain vitality if they last too long, fail to engage employees or turn into one-sided lectures. The conservation of resources theory, originally proposed in 1989 by Dr. Stevan Hobfoll, states that psychological stress occurs when a person's resources are threatened or lost. When resources are low, a person will shift into defence to conserve their remaining supply. In the case of office meetings, where some of employees' most valuable resources are their focus, alertness and motivation, this can mean an abrupt halt in productivity as they take time to recover.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`As humans, when we transition from one task to another on the job—say from sitting in a meeting to doing normal work—it takes an effortful cognitive switch. We must detach ourselves from the previous task and expend significant mental energy to move on. If we are already drained to dangerous levels, then making the mental switch to the next thing is extra tough. It's common to see people cyber-loafing after a frustrating meeting, going and getting coffee, interrupting a colleague and telling them about the meeting, and so on.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Each person's ability to recover from horrible meetings is different. Some can bounce back quickly, while others carry their fatigue until the end of the workday. Yet while no formal MRS studies are currently underway, one can loosely speculate on the length of an average employee's lag time. Switching tasks in a non-MRS condition takes about 10 to 15 minutes. With MRS, it may take as long as 45 minutes on average. It's even worse when a worker has several meetings that are separated by 30 minutes. "Not enough time to transition in a non-MRS situation to get anything done, and in an MRS situation, not quite enough time to recover for the next meeting," says researcher Joseph Allen. "Then, add the compounding of back-to-back bad meetings and we may have an epidemic on our hands."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`In an effort to combat the side effects of MRS, Allen, along with researcher Joseph Mroz and colleagues at the University of Nebraska-Omaha, published a study detailing the best ways to avoid common traps, including a concise checklist of do's and don'ts applicable to any workplace. Drawing from around 200 papers to compile their comprehensive list, Mroz and his team may now hold a remedy to the largely undefined problem of MRS.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Mroz says a good place to start is asking ourselves if our meetings are even necessary in the first place. If all that's on the agenda is a quick catch-up, or some non-urgent information sharing, it may better suit the group to send around an email instead. "The second thing I would always recommend is keep the meeting as small as possible," says Mroz. "If they don't actually have some kind of immediate input, then they can follow up later. They don't need to be sitting in this hour-long meeting." Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to more employee engagement in the meetings they do attend, which experts agree is a proven remedy for MRS.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`Employees also feel taxed when they are invited together to meetings that don't inspire participation, says Cliff Scott, professor of organisational science. It takes precious time for them to vent their emotions, complain and try to regain focus after a pointless meeting—one of the main traps of MRS. Over time as employees find themselves tied up in more and more unnecessary meetings—and thus dealing with increasing lag times from MRS—the waste of workday hours can feel insulting.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Despite the relative scarcity of research behind the subject, Hartman has taught herself many of the same tricks suggested in Mroz's study, and has come a long way since her days of being stuck with unnecessary meetings. The people she invites to meetings today include not just the essential employees, but also representatives from every department that might have a stake in the issue at hand. Managers like her, who seek input even from non-experts to shape their decisions, can find greater support and cooperation from their workforce, she says.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`If an organisation were to apply all 22 suggestions from Mroz and Allen's findings, the most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on the schedule, Mroz says. Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to increased productivity, which is the ultimate objective of convening a meeting. While none of the counter-MRS ideas have been tested empirically yet, Allen says one trick with promise is for employees to identify things that quickly change their mood from negative to positive. As simple as it sounds, finding a personal happy place, going there and then coming straight back to work might be key to facilitating recovery.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`Leaders should see also themselves as "stewards of everyone else's valuable time", adds Steven Rogelberg, author of The Surprising Science of Meetings. Having the skills to foresee potential traps and treat employees' endurance with care allows leaders to provide effective short-term deterrents to MRS.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`Most important, however, is for organisations to awaken to the concept of meetings being flexible, says Allen. By reshaping the way they prioritise employees' time, companies can eliminate the very sources of MRS in their tracks.`}]},i={"cet6-2024-06-s1":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today's world." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today's world. In the digital age, being proficient in digital skills is no longer a luxury but a necessity. The rapid advancement of technology has made digital literacy a fundamental requirement for personal and professional success.
For instance, in the workplace, digital skills enable employees to work more efficiently and effectively. They can use software to manage tasks, collaborate with colleagues remotely, and analyze data to make informed decisions. Moreover, digital literacy is essential for students who need to access online resources, participate in virtual classes, and conduct research using digital tools. Personal experiences also underscore the significance of digital skills. I remember struggling with basic computer operations when I first started college, but as I honed my digital skills, I found myself more capable and confident in my academic and extracurricular activities.
In conclusion, digital literacy and skills are indispensable in our increasingly digital world. They not only enhance our ability to navigate the digital landscape but also empower us to contribute meaningfully to society.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today's world." You can make comment…。参考范文约 180 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：Moreover / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today's world." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today's world." You can make comment…。参考范文约 180 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`Thank you for meeting with me, Stephen, at such short notice. Not a problem, Margaret. Now, please give me some good news. Have you agreed to my last proposal? I have indeed, and I wish to sign the agreement, pending one small change to be made to the contract. Margaret, we have been through this for almost a year now, back and forth making alterations. Are you sure you want to make a sponsorship deal for your client or not? I ask this because, frankly, some people at my end are running out of patience. I understand your concerns, but as I'm sure you understand, we hold our client's best interests to be of the utmost concern. We therefore comb through the fine details of all contracts. Rest assured, we all appreciate your firm's patience. Okay, fine. So, what changes do you wish to make? Essentially, we would like the new deal to exclude the Middle East, that's all. The Middle East, why? My client has a couple of other prospective marketing deals from companies in the Middle East. Those offers, should they materialise, would exclusively employ my client's image in the Middle East only. Therefore, in order to avoid any conflict, we would need to ensure that both marketing campaigns do not overlap geographically. What business sector in the Middle East are we talking about here? Real estate. Well, that should be okay then. So long as the product is very different from our food and beverage market, there should be no conflict of interest. Nevertheless, I will have to run this through my people. I don't foresee any problem, though. The Middle East is a negligible market for us, but I still need to check this with a couple of departments.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`Next, we have a special science-related news story. Paula Hancock is at the Denver Observatory. Paula, what is the big story over there? Hi, John. Yes, all the astronomers on site here are very excited. In fact, space enthusiasts all across North America and the rest of the Northern Hemisphere will be congregating on mountaintops tonight to watch the night sky. Why? What's the big event? Is there an eclipse happening soon? Tonight, the Earth will come into close proximity with the Oppenheimer comet. It is the closest our planet has been to such a phenomenon in over 100 years. For this reason, it is expected that thousands of people will gaze up at the sky tonight in order to see this formidable object. How far away is this comet? Will people be able to see it with the naked eye? The Oppenheimer comet will still be millions of miles away, on the edge of our galaxy. But, nevertheless, this is a relatively close distance, close enough for people to observe in good detail through a telescope. People will only see a blur without one. However, that does not mean one needs professional equipment. Even the most ordinary of telescopes should be conducive for people to observe and wonder at this flying object. Many of our viewers will be wondering how they too can take part in this once -in-a-lifetime event. Where will this comet be in the sky? How can people find it? The comet will be almost exactly due north, at 60 degrees above the equator. However, finding the comet is indeed very tricky, and scientists here have told me there are plenty of phone apps that will facilitate this. How fantastic! Thank you, Paula, for the information.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`Dietary guidelines form the basis for nutrition advice and regulations around the world. While there is strong scientific consensus around most existing guidelines, one question has recently stirred debate. Should consumers be warned to avoid ultra-processed foods? Two papers published today in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition outline the case for and against using the concept of ultra-processed foods to help inform dietary guidelines beyond conventional food classification systems. The authors, Carlos Monteiro of the University of Sao Paulo and Arne Ostrup of Novo Nordisk Foundation, will discuss the issue in a live virtual debate, August 14th, during Nutrition 2024 Live Online. The debate centers around a system developed by Monteiro and colleagues that classifies foods by their degree of industrial processing, ranging from unprocessed to ultra-processed. The system defines ultra-processed foods as those made using sequences of processes that extract substances from foods and alter them with chemicals in order to formulate the final product. Ultra-processed foods are characteristically designed to be cheap, tasty, and convenient. Examples include soft drinks and candy, packaged snacks and pastries, ready-to-heat products, and reconstituted meat products. Studies have linked consumption of ultra-processed foods, which are often high in salt, sugar, and fat, with weight gain and an increased risk of chronic diseases, even after adjusting for the amount of salt, sugar, and fat in the diet. While the mechanisms behind these associations are not fully understood, Monteiro argues that the existing evidence is sufficient to justify discouraging consumption of ultra -processed foods in dietary recommendations and government policies.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-4`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:12,text:`Believe it or not, human creativity benefits from constraints. According to psychologists, when you have less to work with, you actually begin to see the world differently. With constraints, you dedicate your mental energy to acting more resourcefully. When challenged, you figure out new ways to be better. The most successful creative people know that constraints give their minds the impetus to leap higher. People who invent new products are not limited by what they don't have or can't do. They leverage their limitations to push themselves even further. Many products and services are created because the founders saw a limitation in what they use. They created innovation based on what was not working for them at the moment. Innovation is a creative person's response to limitation. In a 2015 study which examined how thinking about scarcity or abundance influences how creatively people use their resources, Ravi Mehta at the University of Illinois and Meng Zhu at Johns Hopkins University found that people simply have no incentive to use what's available to them in novel ways. When people face scarcity, they give themselves the freedom to use resources in less conventional ways because they have to. Obstacles can broaden your perception and open up your thinking processes. Consistent constraints help you improve at connecting unrelated ideas and concepts. Marissa Meyer, former vice president for search products and user experience at Google, once wrote in a publication on Bloomberg, Constraints shape and focus problems and provide clear challenges to overcome. Creativity thrives best when constrained.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-5`,label:`Passage 1 (2)`,questionNo:16,text:`Different people use different strategies for managing conflicts. These strategies are learned in childhood. Usually, we are not aware of how we act in conflict situations. We just do whatever seems to come naturally. But, we do have a personal strategy, and because it is learned, we can always change it by learning new and more effective ways of managing conflicts. When you get involved in a conflict, there are two major concerns you have to take into account. Achieving your personal goals, and keeping a good relationship with the other person. How important your personal goals are, and how important the relationship is to you, affect how you act in a conflict. Given these two concerns, five styles of managing conflicts can be identified. 1. The Turtle. Turtles withdraw into their shells to avoid conflicts. They give up their personal goals and relationships. They believe it is easier to withdraw from a conflict than to face it. 2. The Shark. Sharks try to overpower opponents by forcing them to accept their solution to the conflict. They seek to achieve their goals at all costs. Sharks assume that conflicts are settled by one person winning and one person losing. Winning gives sharks a sense of pride and achievement. Losing gives them a sense of weakness, inadequacy, and failure. 3. The Teddy Bear. Teddy bears want to be accepted and liked by other people. They think that conflict should be avoided in favor of harmony, and believe that conflicts cannot be discussed without damaging relationships. They give up their goals to preserve the relationship. 4. The Fox. Foxes are moderately concerned with their own goals and about their relationships with other people. They give up part of their goals and persuade the other person in a conflict to give up part of his goals. They seek a solution to conflicts where both sides gain something. 5. The Owl. Owls view conflicts as problems to be solved. They see conflicts as improving relationships by reducing tension between two people. They try to begin a discussion that identifies the conflict as a problem. By seeking solutions that satisfy both themselves and the other person, owls maintain the relationship. Owls are not satisfied until a solution is found that achieves their own goals and the other person's goals, and they are not satisfied until the tensions and negative feelings have been fully resolved.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-6`,label:`Passage 2 (2)`,questionNo:19,text:`The genetic code of all 1.5 million known species of animals and plants living on Earth will be mapped to help save species from extinction and boost human health. Scientists hope that cracking the genetic code of plants and animals could help uncover new treatments for infectious diseases, slow aging, improve crops and agriculture, and create new biomaterials. In Britain, organisations including the Natural History Museum, the Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew, and the Wellcome Sanger Institute have joined forces to sequence Britain's 66,000 species of animals and plants. Dubbed the Darwin Tree of Life Project, it is expected to take 10 years and cost £100 million. Once completed, all the information will be publicly available to researchers. Many scientists believe that Earth has now entered the sixth mass extinction, with humans creating a toxic mix of habitat loss, pollution and climate change, which has already led to the loss of at least 77 species of mammals and 140 types of birds since 1500. It is the biggest loss of species since the dinosaurs were wiped out 66 million years ago. Scientists say that sequencing every species will revolutionise the understanding of biology and evolution, bolster efforts to conserve as well as protect and restore biodiversity. Dr Tim Littlewood, Head of Life Sciences Department at the Natural History Museum said, Whether you are interested in food or disease, the history of how every organism on the planet has adapted to its environment is recorded in its genetic make-up. How you then harness that is dependent on your ability to understand it. We will be using modern methods to get a really good window on the present and the past. And of course, a window on the past gives you a prospective model on the future. Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome said, Try as I might, I can't think of a more exciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational project. Since 1970, humanity has wiped out 60% of animal populations. About 23,000 of 80,000 species surveyed are approaching extinction. We are in the midst of the sixth great extinction event of life on our planet, which not only threatens wildlife species, but also imperils the global food supply. As scientists, we all realise we desperately need to catalogue life on our fragile planet now. I think we're making history.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-ls-7`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`John Donne, the English poet, wrote in the 17th century No man is an island entire of itself. Every man is a piece of the continent, a part of the main. Now, a British academic has claimed that human individuality is indeed just an illusion because societies are far more interconnected at a mental, physical and cultural level than people realise. In his new book, The Self-Delusion, Professor Tom Oliver, a researcher in the Ecology and Evolution group at the University of Reading, argues there is no such thing as self and not even our bodies are truly us. Just as Copernicus realised the Earth is not the centre of the universe, Professor Oliver said society urgently needs a Copernican-like revolution to understand people are not detached beings but rather part of one connected identity. A significant milestone in the cultural evolution of human minds was the acceptance that the Earth is not the centre of the universe, the so-called Copernican Revolution, he writes. However, we have one more big myth to dispose of, that we exist as independent selves at the centre of a subjective universe. You may feel as if you are an independent individual acting autonomously in the world, that you have unchanging inner self that persists throughout your lifetime, acting as a central anchor point with the world changing around you. This is the illusion I seek to tackle. We are intimately connected to the world around us. Professor Oliver argues there are around 37 trillion cells in the body but most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks, so the material us is constantly changing. In fact, there is no part of your body that has existed for more than 10 years. Since our bodies are essentially made anew every few weeks, the material in them alone is clearly insufficient. To explain the persistent thread of an identity, Professor Oliver claims that individualism is actually bad for society and only by realizing we are part of a bigger entity can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems. Through selfish overconsumption we are destroying the natural world and using non-renewable resources at an accelerating rate. We are at a critical crossroads as a species where we must rapidly reform our mindsets and behavior to act in less selfish ways, he said. So let's open our eyes to the hidden connections all around us.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she will do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Reply to the man's last proposal within a short time.`},{key:`B`,text:`Sign the agreement if one small change is made to it.`},{key:`C`,text:`Make a sponsorship deal for her client at the meeting.`},{key:`D`,text:`Give the man some good news regarding the contract.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I have indeed, and I wish to sign the agreement, pending one small change to be made to the contract."。正确选项为 "Sign the agreement if one small change is made to it."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about some people he represents?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are becoming impatient.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are afraid time is running out.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are used to making alterations.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are concerned about the details.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I ask this because, frankly, some people at my end are running out of patience."。正确选项为 "They are becoming impatient."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What reason does the woman give for the new deal to exclude the Middle East?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`To prevent geographical discrimination.`},{key:`B`,text:`To tap the food and beverage market.`},{key:`C`,text:`To avoid any conflict of interest.`},{key:`D`,text:`To reduce unfair competition.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："Essentially, we would like the new deal to exclude the Middle East, that's all."。正确选项为 "To avoid any conflict of interest."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say about the Middle East?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is a potential market for food and beverage.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is very attractive for real estate developers.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is a negligible market for his company.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is very different from other markets.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："The Middle East is a negligible market for us, but I still need to check this with a couple of departments."。正确选项为 "It is a negligible market for his company."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say about all the astronomers at the Denver Observatory?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are thrilled by a rare astronomic phenomenon.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are celebrating a big event on mountain tops.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are enthusiastic about big science-related stories.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are joined by astronomers all across North America.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："Paula Hancock is at the Denver Observatory."。正确选项为 "They are thrilled by a rare astronomic phenomenon."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the conversation about the Oppenheimer comet?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It will be the most formidable of its kind in over a century.`},{key:`B`,text:`It will come closest to Earth in more than one hundred years.`},{key:`C`,text:`It will eclipse many other such events in human history.`},{key:`D`,text:`It will be seen most clearly from Denver's mountain tops.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："Tonight, the Earth will come into close proximity with the Oppenheimer comet."。正确选项为 "It will come closest to Earth in more than one hundred years."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say people will only see in the sky without a telescope?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A blur.`},{key:`B`,text:`Stars.`},{key:`C`,text:`The edge of our galaxy.`},{key:`D`,text:`An ordinary flying object.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："People will only see a blur without one."。正确选项为 "A blur."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`What do scientists at the Denver Observatory advise amateurs do to facilitate their observation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Use professional equipment.`},{key:`B`,text:`Climb to the nearby heights.`},{key:`C`,text:`Fix their eyes due north.`},{key:`D`,text:`Make use of phone apps.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："However, finding the comet is indeed very tricky, and scientists here have told me there are plenty of phone apps that will facilitate this."。正确选项为 "Make use of phone apps."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What question is said to have recently stirred debate?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Whether consumers should be warned against ultra-processed foods.`},{key:`B`,text:`Whether there is sufficient scientific consensus on dietary guidelines.`},{key:`C`,text:`Whether guidelines can form the basis for nutrition advice to consumers.`},{key:`D`,text:`Whether food scientists will agree on the concept of ultra-processed foods.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："While there is strong scientific consensus around most existing guidelines, one question has recently stirred debate."。正确选项为 "Whether consumers should be warned against ultra-processed foods."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`How does the system developed by Monteiro and colleagues classify foods?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`By the labor cost for the final products.`},{key:`B`,text:`By the degree of industrial processing.`},{key:`C`,text:`By the extent of chemical alteration.`},{key:`D`,text:`By the convention of classification.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："The debate centers around a system developed by Monteiro and colleagues that classifies foods by their degree of industrial processing, ranging from unprocessed to ultra-processed."。正确选项为 "By the degree of industrial processing."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What is consumption of ultra-processed foods linked with, according to studies?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Increased consumers' expenses.`},{key:`B`,text:`Greater risk of chronic diseases.`},{key:`C`,text:`People's misunderstanding of nutrition.`},{key:`D`,text:`Children's dislike for unprocessed foods.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："Studies have linked consumption of ultra-processed foods, which are often high in salt, sugar, and fat, with weight gain and an increased risk of chronic diseases, even after adjusting for the amount of salt, sugar, and fat in the diet."。正确选项为 "Greater risk of chronic diseases."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What do psychologists say people do when they are short of resources?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They begin to think of the benefits of constraints.`},{key:`B`,text:`They try to seek solutions from creative people.`},{key:`C`,text:`They try hard to maximize their mental energy.`},{key:`D`,text:`They begin to see the world in a different way.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："According to psychologists, when you have less to work with, you actually begin to see the world differently."。正确选项为 "They begin to see the world in a different way."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about innovation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is characteristic of all creative people.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is essential to pushing society forward.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is a creative person's response to limitation.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is an impetus to socio-economic development.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："Innovation is a creative person's response to limitation."。正确选项为 "It is a creative person's response to limitation."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What did a 2015 study by Ravi Mehta and Meng Zhu find?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Scarcity or abundance of resources has little impact on people's creativity.`},{key:`B`,text:`Innovative people are not constrained in connecting unrelated concepts.`},{key:`C`,text:`People have no incentive to use available resources in new ways.`},{key:`D`,text:`Creative people tend to consume more available resources.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："In a 2015 study which examined how thinking about scarcity or abundance influences how creatively people use their resources, Ravi Mehta at the University of Illinois and Meng Zhu at Johns Hopkins University found that people simply have no incentive to use wh…"。正确选项为 "People have no incentive to use available resources in new ways."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What did Marissa Meyer once write concerning creativity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is key to a company's survival.`},{key:`B`,text:`It shapes and focuses problems.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is essential to meeting challenges.`},{key:`D`,text:`It thrives best when constrained.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："Creativity thrives best when constrained."。正确选项为 "It thrives best when constrained."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the speaker say strategies for managing conflicts can always be changed?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Because they are learned.`},{key:`B`,text:`Because they come naturally.`},{key:`C`,text:`Because they have to be properly personalized.`},{key:`D`,text:`Because there can be more effective strategies.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："But, we do have a personal strategy, and because it is learned, we can always change it by learning new and more effective ways of managing conflicts."。正确选项为 "Because they are learned."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What is said to affect the way one acts in a conflict?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The extent of difference and of similarity between the two sides.`},{key:`B`,text:`The knowledge of the specific expectation the other side holds.`},{key:`C`,text:`The importance of one's goals and of the relationship.`},{key:`D`,text:`The approaches one adopts to conflict management.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："How important your personal goals are, and how important the relationship is to you, affect how you act in a conflict."。正确选项为 "The importance of one's goals and of the relationship."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`Of the five styles the speaker discusses, which views conflicts as problems to be solved?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The fox.`},{key:`B`,text:`The owl.`},{key:`C`,text:`The shark.`},{key:`D`,text:`The turtle.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："Given these two concerns, five styles of managing conflicts can be identified."。正确选项为 "The owl."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What do scientists hope to do by cracking the genetic code of plants and animals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Help save species from extinction and boost human health.`},{key:`B`,text:`Understand how plants and animals perished over the past.`},{key:`C`,text:`Help gather information publicly available to researchers.`},{key:`D`,text:`Find out the cause of extinction of Britain's 66,000 species.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："5 million known species of animals and plants living on Earth will be mapped to help save species from extinction and boost human health."。正确选项为 "Help save species from extinction and boost human health."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What do many scientists believe with regard to Earth?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was once dominated by dinosaurs.`},{key:`B`,text:`It has entered the sixth mass extinction.`},{key:`C`,text:`Its prospects depend on future human behaviour.`},{key:`D`,text:`Its climate change is aggravated by humans.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："Many scientists believe that Earth has now entered the sixth mass extinction, with humans creating a toxic mix of habitat loss, pollution and climate change, which has already led to the loss of at least 77 species of mammals and 140 types of birds since 1500."。正确选项为 "It has entered the sixth mass extinction."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`How does Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome, describe the Darwin Tree of Life project?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It dwarfs all other efforts to conserve, protect and restore biodiversity on earth.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is costly to get started and requires the joint efforts of thousands of scientists.`},{key:`C`,text:`It can help to bring back the large numbers of plants and animals that have gone extinct.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is the most exciting, most relevant, most timely and most internationally inspirational.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2 (2)）依据："Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome said, Try as I might, I can't think of a more exciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational project."。正确选项为 "It is the most exciting, most relevant, most timely and most internationally inspirational."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What is indeed just an illusion according to Professor Tom Oliver?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Cultural identity.`},{key:`B`,text:`Social evolution.`},{key:`C`,text:`The Copernican revolution.`},{key:`D`,text:`Human individuality.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："Now, a British academic has claimed that human individuality is indeed just an illusion because societies are far more interconnected at a mental, physical and cultural level than people realise."。正确选项为 "Human individuality."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What does Professor Tom Oliver think of the idea that we exist as independent selves at the center of a subjective universe?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is a delusion to be disposed of.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is prevalent even among academics.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is a myth spread by John Donne's poem.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is rooted in the mindset of the 17th century.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："However, we have one more big myth to dispose of, that we exist as independent selves at the centre of a subjective universe."。正确选项为 "It is a delusion to be disposed of."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`Why does Professor Tom Oliver claim that the material us is constantly changing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`He believes in Copernican philosophical doctrines about the universe.`},{key:`B`,text:`He has gained ample scientific evidence at the University of Reading.`},{key:`C`,text:`He has found that our inner self and material self are interconnected.`},{key:`D`,text:`He contends most of our body cells can only live a few days or weeks.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："Professor Oliver argues there are around 37 trillion cells in the body but most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks, so the material us is constantly changing."。正确选项为 "He contends most of our body cells can only live a few days or weeks."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`How can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems according to Professor Tom Oliver? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`By coming to see how disruptive such problems have got to be.`},{key:`B`,text:`By realising that we all can do our own bit in such endeavours.`},{key:`C`,text:`By becoming aware that we are part of a bigger world.`},{key:`D`,text:`By making joint efforts resolutely and persistently.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 3）依据："To explain the persistent thread of an identity, Professor Oliver claims that individualism is actually bad for society and only by realizing we are part of a bigger entity can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems."。正确选项为 "By becoming aware that we are part of a bigger world."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`alleviate`,B:`clarity`,C:`cognitive`,D:`composers`,E:`hurt`,F:`inhibiting`,G:`interrogation`,H:`intrinsically`,I:`loose`,J:`majestic`,K:`mandatory`,L:`recover`,M:`significantly`,N:`soothing`,O:`wards`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-cp-1`,text:`It's quite remarkable how different genres of music can spark unique feelings, emotions, and memories. Studies have shown that music can reduce stress and anxiety before surgeries and we are all attracted toward our own unique life soundtrack. If you're looking to The sounds of classical music produce a calming effect letting Although there are many great It has been found that listening to classical music Whether classical music is something that you listen to on a regular basis or not, it wouldn't`}]},t,{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1`,title:`Passage One`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-1`,text:`It is irrefutable that employees know the difference between right and wrong. So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-2`,text:`There are a number of factors that influence whether people intervene. First, they need to be able to see a risky situation beginning to unfold. Second, the company's culture needs to make them feel safe to speak up. And third, they need to have the communication skills to say something effectively.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-3`,text:`This is not strictly a workplace problem; it's a growing problem off the job too. Every day people witness things on the street and choose to stand idly by. This is known as the bystander effect—the more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone in that group is to help the victim. The psychology behind this is called diffusion of responsibility. Basically, the larger the crowd, the more people assume that someone else will take care of it—meaning no one effectively intervenes or acts in a moment of need.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-4`,text:`This crowd mentality is strong enough for people to evade their known responsibilities. But it's not only frontline workers who don't make safety interventions in the workplace. There are also instances where supervisors do not intervene either.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-5`,text:`When a group of employees sees unsafe behavior not being addressed at a leadership level it creates the precedent that this is how these situations should be addressed, thus defining the safety culture for everyone.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-6`,text:`Despite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time. Fear is the ultimate factor in not intervening. There is a fear of penalty, a fear that they'll have to do more work if they intervene. Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-7`,text:`On many worksites, competent workers must be appointed. Part of their job is to intervene when workers perform a task without the proper equipment or if the conditions are unsafe. Competent workers are also required to stop work from continuing when there's a danger.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-8`,text:`Supervisors also play a critical role. Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p1-9`,text:`Beyond competent workers and supervisors, it's important to educate everyone within the organization that they are obliged to intervene if they witness a possible unsafe act, whether you're a designated competent person, a supervisor or a frontline worker.`}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2`,title:`Passage Two`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-1`,text:`The term "environmentalist" can mean different things. It used to refer to people trying to protect wildlife and natural ecosystems. In the 21st century, the term has evolved to capture the need to combat human-made climate change.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-2`,text:`The distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-3`,text:`On one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy. The opposing side agrees that renewables are crucial, but says society needs an amount of power available to meet consumers' basic demands when the sun isn't shining and the wind isn't blowing. Nuclear energy, being far cleaner than oil, gas and coal, is a natural option, especially where hydroelectric capacity is limited.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-4`,text:`Leon Clarke, who helped author reports for the UN's Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, isn't an uncritical supporter of nuclear energy, but says it's a valuable option to have if we're serious about reaching carbon neutrality.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-5`,text:`"Core to all of this is the degree to which you think we can actually meet climate goals with 100% renewables," he said. "If you don't believe we can do it, and you care about the climate, you are forced to think about something like nuclear."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-6`,text:`The achievability of universal 100% renewability is similarly contentious. Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been "100% renewable" for years. But these cities often have small populations, occasionally still rely on fossil fuel energy and have significant renewable resources at their immediate disposal. Meanwhile, countries that manage to run off renewables typically do so thanks to extraordinary hydroelectric capabilities.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-7`,text:`Germany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy. Chancellor Angela Merkel in 2011 announced Energiewende, an energy transition that would phase out nuclear and coal while phasing in renewables. Wind and solar power generation has increased over 400% since 2010, and renewables provided 46% of the country's electricity in 2019.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-8`,text:`But progress has halted in recent years. The instability of renewables doesn't just mean energy is often not produced at night, but also that solar and wind can overwhelm the grid during the day, forcing utilities to pay customers to use their electricity. Lagging grid infrastructure struggles to transport this overabundance of green energy from Germany's north to its industrial south, meaning many factories still run on coal and gas. The political limit has also been reached in some places, with citizens meeting the construction of new wind turbines with loud protests.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-p2-9`,text:`The result is that Germany's greenhouse gas emissions have fallen by around 11.5% since 2010—slower than the EU average of 13.5%.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（alleviate）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`I`,explanation:`答案 I（loose）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`B`,explanation:`答案 B（clarity）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`D`,explanation:`答案 D（composers）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`C`,explanation:`答案 C（cognitive）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`O`,explanation:`答案 O（wards）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`M`,explanation:`答案 M（significantly）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`L`,explanation:`答案 L（recover）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`E`,explanation:`答案 E（hurt）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`N`,explanation:`答案 N（soothing）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Jackson was said to have transferred his ownership of the oak tree to itself in order to protect it from being destroyed.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。C 段："Towards this end, Jackson transferred by means of a deed ownership of the tree and a little land around it to the tree itself."，与题干陈述 "Jackson was said to have transferred his ownership of the oak tree to itself in order to protect it from being destroyed." 信息对应，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`No proof has been found from an extensive search that Jackson had ever owned the land where the oak tree grew.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`正确答案 H。H 段："Further, an extensive search of land ownership records in Athens does not seem to indicate Jackson ever owned the land the tree sits on."，与题干陈述 "No proof has been found from an extensive search that Jackson had ever owned the land where the oak tree grew." 信息对应，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`When it was raining heavily, Jackson often took shelter under a big tree that is said to own itself.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。B 段："Sometime in the 19th century a Georgian called Colonel William Jackson reportedly took a liking to the said tree and endeavored to protect it from any danger."，与题干陈述 "When it was raining heavily, Jackson often took shelter under a big tree that is said to own itself." 信息对应，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`There is no evidence that Jackson had made arrangements to pay property taxes for the land on which the oak tree sat.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段："And whatever the case there, Jackson would have known property taxes needed to be paid on the deeded land for the tree to be truly secure in its future."，与题干陈述 "There is no evidence that Jackson had made arrangements to pay property taxes for the land on which the oak tree sat." 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Professor Ward paid Jackson over one thousand dollars when purchasing a piece of land from him.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`正确答案 I。I 段："Whatever the case, in 1832 a four acre parcel, which included the land the tree was on and the neighboring land Jackson lived on, among others, was sold to University professor Malthus A Ward."，与题干陈述 "Professor Ward paid Jackson over one thousand dollars when purchasing a piece of land from him." 信息对应，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`It is said the tree that owned itself fell in a heavy windstorm.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："Finally, on October 9, 1942, the over 30 meter tall and 200-400 year old tree fell, rumor has it, as a result of a severe windstorm and/or via having previously died and its roots rotted."，与题干陈述 "It is said the tree that owned itself fell in a heavy windstorm." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`The story of the oak tree is suspected to have been invented as a thought exercise.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`正确答案 N。N 段："As to why the author would do this, it's speculated perhaps it was a 19th century version of a click-bait thought exercise on whether it would be legal for someone to deed such a non-conscious living thing to itself or not."，与题干陈述 "The story of the oak tree is suspected to have been invented as a thought exercise." 信息对应，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`Jackson's little plantation was auctioned off to settle his debt in the mid-19th century.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`正确答案 J。J 段："Ten years later, in 1844, Jackson seemed to have come into financial difficulties and had his little plantation seized by the Clarke County Sheriff's office and auctioned off to settle the mortgage."，与题干陈述 "Jackson's little plantation was auctioned off to settle his debt in the mid-19th century." 信息对应，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`An official ceremony was held to celebrate the transplanting of a small tree to where its ancestor had stood.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`正确答案 E。E 段："A couple of months later, an official ceremony was held featuring none other than the Mayor of Athens, Robert L McWhorter, to commemorate the occasion."，与题干陈述 "An official ceremony was held to celebrate the transplanting of a small tree to where its ancestor had stood." 信息对应，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`The story of the Tree That Owns Itself appeared in the local paper several times, with slight alterations in wording.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`O`,explanation:`正确答案 O。O 段："Whatever the case, the next known instance of the Tree That Owns Itself being mentioned wasn't until 1901 in the Centennial Edition of that same paper, the Athens Weekly Banner."，与题干陈述 "The story of the Tree That Owns Itself appeared in the local paper several times, with slight alterations in wording." 信息对应，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the factors contributing to failure of intervention in face of risky behavior in the workplace?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Slack supervision style.`},{key:`B`,text:`Unfavorable workplace culture.`},{key:`C`,text:`Unforeseeable risk.`},{key:`D`,text:`Blocked communication.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?"。正确选项为 "Unfavorable workplace culture."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author mean by "diffusion of responsibility" (Line 4, Para. 3)?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The more people are around, the more they need to worry about their personal safety.`},{key:`B`,text:`The more people who witness an event, the less likely anyone will venture to participate.`},{key:`C`,text:`The more people idling around on the street, the more likely they need taking care of.`},{key:`D`,text:`The more people are around, the less chance someone will step forward to intervene.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："Unsuccessful attempts in the past are another strong contributing factor to why people don't intervene—they tend to prefer to defer that action to someone else for all future situations."。正确选项为 "The more people are around, the less chance someone will step forward to intervene."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What happens when unsafe behavior at the workplace is not addressed by the leaders?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`No one will intervene when they see similar behaviors.`},{key:`B`,text:`Everyone will see it as the easiest way to deal with crisis.`},{key:`C`,text:`Workers have to take extra caution executing their duties.`},{key:`D`,text:`Workers are left to take care of the emergency themselves.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："So why don't more employees intervene when they see someone exhibiting at-risk behavior in the workplace?"。正确选项为 "No one will intervene when they see similar behaviors."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What is the ultimate reason workers won't act when they see unsafe operations?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Preference of deferring the action to others.`},{key:`B`,text:`Anticipation of leadership intervention.`},{key:`C`,text:`Fear of being isolated by coworkers.`},{key:`D`,text:`Fear of having to do more work.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："Despite the fact that workers are encouraged to intervene when they observe unsafe operations, this happens less than half of the time."。正确选项为 "Fear of having to do more work."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What is critical to ensuring workplace safety?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Workers are trained to operate their equipment properly.`},{key:`B`,text:`Workers exhibiting at-risk behavior are strictly disciplined.`},{key:`C`,text:`Supervisors create a safety environment for timely intervention.`},{key:`D`,text:`Supervisors conduct effective communication with frontline workers.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage One）："Even if a competent person isn't required, supervisors need a broad set of skills to not only identify and alleviate workplace hazards but also build a safety climate within their team that supports intervening and open communication among them."。正确选项为 "Supervisors create a safety environment for timely intervention."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What accounts for the divide within the scientific community about nuclear energy?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Attention to combating human-made climate change.`},{key:`B`,text:`Emphasis on protecting wildlife and natural ecosystems.`},{key:`C`,text:`Evolution of the term 'green energy' over the last century.`},{key:`D`,text:`Adherence to different interpretations of environmentalism.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）："The distinction between these two strands of environmentalism is the cause of a split within the scientific community about nuclear energy."。正确选项为 "Adherence to different interpretations of environmentalism."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What is the solution to energy shortage proposed by purists' opponents?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Relying on renewables firmly and exclusively.`},{key:`B`,text:`Using fossil fuel and green energy alternately.`},{key:`C`,text:`Opting for nuclear energy when necessary.`},{key:`D`,text:`Limiting people's non-basic consumption.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage Two）："On one side are purists who believe nuclear power isn't worth the risk and the exclusive solution to the climate crisis is renewable energy."。正确选项为 "Opting for nuclear energy when necessary."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What point does the author want to make with cities like Burlington as an example?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is controversial whether the goal of the whole world's exclusive dependence on renewables is attainable.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is contentious whether cities with large populations have renewable resources at their immediate disposal.`},{key:`C`,text:`It is arguable whether cities that manage to run off renewables have sustainable hydroelectric capabilities.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is debatable whether traditional fossil fuel energy can be done away with entirely throughout the world.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）："Cities such as Burlington, Vermont, have been "100% renewable" for years."。正确选项为 "It is controversial whether the goal of the whole world's exclusive dependence on renewables is attainable."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Germany regarding renewable energy?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It has increased its wind and solar power generation four times over the last two decades.`},{key:`B`,text:`It represents a good example of a major industrialized country promoting green energy.`},{key:`C`,text:`It relies on renewable energy to generate more than half of its electricity.`},{key:`D`,text:`It has succeeded in reaching the goal of energy transition set by Merkel.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage Two）："Germany stands as the best case study for a large, industrialized country pushing into green energy."。正确选项为 "It represents a good example of a major industrialized country promoting green energy."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What may be one of the reasons for Germany's progress having halted in recent years?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Its grid infrastructure's capacity has fallen behind its development of green energy.`},{key:`B`,text:`Its overabundance of green energy has forced power plants to suspend operation during daytime.`},{key:`C`,text:`Its industrial south is used to running factories on conventional energy supplies.`},{key:`D`,text:`Its renewable energy supplies are unstable both at night and during the day.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）："But progress has halted in recent years."。正确选项为 "Its grid infrastructure's capacity has fallen behind its development of green energy."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`China's traditional wedding customs have a long history, gradually forming a complete set of wedding ceremonies since the Zhou Dynasty, some of which are still in use today. Modern Chinese wedding customs have changed significantly, but the wedding celebrations remain very grand. The wedding venue is meticulously decorated, with the color red, symbolizing jubilance, as the main theme, and many objects are placed to wish the newlyweds happiness. At the wedding, the couple bow to Heaven and Earth, bow to their parents and each other, then hold a banquet to entertain guests and toast to thank them. Today, many young people still have a fondness for traditional Chinese weddings, experiencing a unique and beautiful Chinese romance.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）中国的传统婚礼习俗历史悠久，从周朝开始就逐渐形成了一套完整的婚礼仪式，有些一直沿用至今。 → China's traditional wedding customs have a long history, gradually forming a complete set of wedding ceremonies since the Zhou Dynasty, some of which…`,`2）如今的中式婚礼习俗已有很大变化，但婚礼庆典仍然十分隆重。 → Modern Chinese wedding customs have changed significantly, but the wedding celebrations remain very grand.`,`3）婚礼场地经过精心装饰，以象征喜庆（jubilance）的红色为主色调，摆放着许多祝愿新人幸福的物件。 → The wedding venue is meticulously decorated, with the color red, symbolizing jubilance, as the main theme, and many objects are placed to wish the new…`,`4）在婚礼上，新人要拜天地（bow to Heaven and Earth），拜父母和相互对拜，然后设宴招待宾客，并向宾客敬酒致谢。 → At the wedding, the couple bow to Heaven and Earth, bow to their parents and each other, then hold a banquet to entertain guests and toast to thank th…`,`5）今天，许多年轻人依然钟情于传统的中式婚礼，体验独特而美好的中国式浪漫。 → Today, many young people still have a fondness for traditional Chinese weddings, experiencing a unique and beautiful Chinese romance.`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s1-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`中国的传统婚礼习俗历史悠久，从周朝开始就逐渐形成了一套完整的婚礼仪式，有些一直沿用至今。如今的中式婚礼习俗已有很大变化，但婚礼庆典仍然十分隆重。婚礼场地经过精心装饰，以象征喜庆（jubilance）的红色为主色调，摆放着许多祝愿新人幸福的物件。在婚礼上，新人要拜天地（bow to Heaven and Earth），拜父母和相互对拜，然后设宴招待宾客，并向宾客敬酒致谢。今天，许多年轻人依然钟情于传统的中式婚礼，体验独特而美好的中国式浪漫。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）中国的传统婚礼习俗历史悠久，从周朝开始就逐渐形成了一套完整的婚礼仪式，有些一直沿用至今。 → China's traditional wedding customs have a long history, gradually forming a complete set of wedding ceremonies since the Zhou Dynasty, some of which… 2）如今的中式婚礼习俗已有很大变化，但婚礼庆典仍然十分隆重。 → Modern Chinese wedding customs have changed significantly, but the wedding celebrations remain very grand. 3）婚礼场地经过精心装饰，以象征喜庆（jubilance）的红色为主色调，摆放着许多祝愿新人幸福的物件。 → The wedding venue is meticulously decorated, with the color red, symbolizing jubilance, as the main theme, and many objects are placed to wish the new… 4）在婚礼上，新人要拜天地（bow to Heaven and Earth），拜父母和相互对拜，然后设宴招待宾客，并向宾客敬酒致谢。 → At the wedding, the couple bow to Heaven and Earth, bow to their parents and each other, then hold a banquet to entertain guests and toast to thank th…`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2606,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2024`,`6 月`,`第 1 套`],summary:`2024 年 6 月大学英语六级真题（1 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2024 年 6 月 · 第 1 套 · 站内可作答`},"cet6-2024-06-s2":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays more and more college students have come to realize social practice and academic learning are equally important." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Nowadays more and more college students have come to realize social practice and academic learning are equally important. This belief stems from the understanding that a well-rounded education involves both theoretical knowledge and practical skills.
In the realm of academic learning, students delve into textbooks and lectures, acquiring the foundational knowledge that forms the basis of their future professions. However, this theoretical framework is often insufficient to prepare them for the complexities of the real world. Social practice, on the other hand, offers a complementary experience. It allows students to apply their academic knowledge in real-life scenarios, fostering critical thinking and problem-solving abilities. For instance, a business student might learn about market analysis in class, but it is through internships and case studies that they truly grasp the dynamics of the market.
In conclusion, the synergy between social practice and academic learning is indispensable for the holistic development of college students. It is through this balance that students can bridge the gap between theory and practice, equipping themselves with the competencies necessary to thrive in their future careers.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays more and more college students have come to realize social practice and academic learning are equally import…。参考范文约 178 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：However / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays more and more college students have come to realize social practice and academic learning are equally important." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays more and more college students have come to realize social practice and academic learning are equally import…。参考范文约 178 词、3 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-ls-1`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:1,text:`I've just bought a new blender. What's that? A blender. You know, a machine that blends food. Ah yes, of course. The electric kitchen appliance. Exactly. This one is state-of-the-art. I've been meaning to buy one for a while, and I did thorough research on which specific model to get. I read through maybe hundreds of online user reviews. Anyway, it's amazing. Really? What could be so special about it? I mean, it's just a blender. Well, basically, it's just a very good one. It feels heavy and sturdy and well-made. It also has lots of power and can easily cut and crush practically anything. This way, the soups and juices I make come out really fine and smooth, with no lumpy bits. Hmm, I see. I've never thought of getting one myself. It sounds like the kind of thing that, for me personally, I would rarely use. I've never had one before, and now that I do, I use it all the time. I make a fresh fruit juice in the morning. Maybe not every morning, but three or four times a week, and it feels fantastic. It's a really healthy habit. I can imagine that must feel quite satisfying. I can picture you getting all creative in the kitchen and trying out a multitude of different ingredients. And it's obviously going to be healthier than buying packaged juice from a supermarket. It's so much healthier. It's not even close. Did you know that store-bought juice is like 10% sugar? Right. So then, you bought it for the health benefits? Mostly yes. Basically, it allows me to have a more varied diet, with a far wider assortment of nutrients. Because it's not only fruit in my morning juices, you see. I can also throw in vegetables, nuts, yogurts, cereals, anything that tickles my fancy.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-ls-2`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:5,text:`Today, we have a very interesting guest. Mr. Thomas Benjamin Grimm, the Mayor of Birkton, is here to talk about his job and responsibilities overseeing this charming village. Mr. Grimm, thank you for being here. Thank you for having me. I'd like to start by stating the obvious. Birkton has become one of the most popular tourist destinations in the country, and this has happened under your watch. Just how did you achieve this? The achievement belongs to all the residents of Birkton. It was a shared effort, where everybody pitched in for the communal good. But how did this change happen? In about ten years, Birkton has gone from a relatively unheard-of, sleepy village to a must-see destination. Yes, the change has truly been remarkable. Birkton was always fortunate to be endowed with such a beautiful, natural allure. The Enbury Hills above the village remain untouched by human development, and the Sonora Valley just below it is equally stunning. The transformation commenced in a town hall meeting in spring 2008 – over ten years ago now – when an overwhelming majority of neighbours voted in favour of Motion 836. This legislative proposal essentially set out to harmonise the aesthetic appearance of all the houses in Birkton. The idea was that if all the properties looked a certain way with shared design features, then a village as a whole would look more beautiful. And it worked. It certainly did. I'm looking now at a before and after photo, and the change is truly remarkable. It's hard to believe it's the same place. And how do the neighbours feel now? Great pride, I would say. But what about the multitudes of visitors now crowding the streets? Is everyone happy about that? The tourists we receive are a blessing, as they have completely revitalised our local economy. Every visitor is warmly welcome.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-ls-3`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:9,text:`Researchers in the US have created a remote -controlled robot that is so small it can walk on the top of a US penny. In research published in the journal Science Robotics, a team at Northwestern University said the crab -like robot is 0.5mm wide. Researchers described it as the smallest ever remote -controlled walking robot. The tiny robot can bend, twist, crawl, walk, turn and even jump without the use of complex hardware or special power. The engineers said this is because the robot is powered by the elastic property of its body. To construct the robot, the researchers used a shape-memory alloy material that transforms to its remembered shape when heated. Using a laser, the team is able to heat the robot at specific parts of its body, causing it to change shape. As the robot deforms and goes back to its original shape, it creates movement from one place to another. Because these structures are so tiny, the rate of cooling is very fast, Project Lead Professor John A. Rogers said. In fact, reducing the sizes of these robots allows them to run faster. While the research is still in the exploratory phase, the team believes that technology could lead to micro-sized robots that can perform practical tasks in tightly confined spaces. You might imagine micro-robots as agents to repair or assemble small structures or machines in industry, or as surgical assistants to clear clogged arteries, to stop internal bleeding, or to eliminate cancerous tumors, all in minimally invasive procedures, Rogers said.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-ls-4`,label:`Passage 1 (2)`,questionNo:16,text:`I don't want to boast or anything, but I have always considered myself something of an elite sleeper. Given the opportunity, I will sleep for marathon stretches and can doze through the most extreme situations. On one very rough ferry crossing, on the route to the Isles of Scilly, for example, my traveling companion spent the entire three-hour ride throwing up in the bathroom, while I dozed happily on a plastic chair. Unfortunately, it has come to my attention that I am not an elite sleeper after all. It seems I am just lazy, because elite sleepers are defined as the approximately 3% of the population who are biologically programmed to need less sleep than the rest of us. According to a study that came out in March, elite sleepers have rare genetic changes, which means they can sleep fewer hours than mere mortals without any risk of cognitive decline. It may not be possible to change your own genes, but can you train yourself to need less sleep? Is there a non-biological way to reach elite sleeper status? I have spent the past year trying to answer that question. Not for fun, I should add, but because having a baby has severely disrupted my sleep, for which I still have a great passion. For a while, I assumed I'd be forced to become one of those people who jump out of bed at the crack of dawn. After a year of tough scientific study, however, I have discovered being forced to get up early in the morning is very different from being an early bird. What do we learn from the passage about elite sleepers? What has the speaker been trying to find out over the past year? What has the speaker discovered after a year of tough scientific study? In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A, B, C and D. Then, mark the corresponding letter on answer sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. If you read an article about a controversial issue, do you think you'd realise if it had changed your beliefs? No one knows your own mind like you do. It seems obvious that you would know if your beliefs had shifted. And yet, a new paper in the Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggests that we actually have very poor awareness of our own belief change, meaning that we will tend to underestimate how much we've been swayed by a convincing article. The researchers recruited over 200 undergraduates across two studies and focused on their beliefs about whether physical punishment of kids is an effective form of discipline. The students reported their initial beliefs about whether physical punishment is an effective way to discipline a child, on a scale from 1, completely disbelieve, to 9, completely believe. Several weeks later, they were given one of two research-based texts to read. Each was several pages long and either presented the arguments and data in favour of physical punishment or against it. After this, the students answered some questions to test their comprehension and memory of the text. Then, the students again scored their belief in whether physical punishment is effective or not. Finally, the researchers asked them to recall what their belief had been at the start of the study. The students' belief about physical punishment changed when they read a text that argued against their own initial position. Crucially, the memory of their initial belief was shifted in the direction of their new belief. In fact, their memory was closer to their current belief than their original belief. The more their belief had changed, the larger this memory bias tended to be, suggesting the students were relying on their current belief to deduce their initial belief. The memory bias was unrelated to the measures of how well they'd understood or recalled the text, suggesting these factors didn't play a role in memory of initial belief or awareness of belief change. The researchers concede that this research was about changes to mostly moderate beliefs. It's likely the findings would be different in the context of changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs. However, our beliefs on most topics are in the moderate range, and as we go about our daily lives reading informative material, these intriguing findings suggest we are mostly ignorant of how what we just read has updated and altered our own position.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-ls-5`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:22,text:`As the American population grows, so does the number of American moms. But more than a century after Mother's Day became an official holiday, even as that number increases, the share of the American population who are mothers is at the lowest point in a quarter century. It's frequently noted that fertility rates are falling sharply in richer countries, but the less observed consequence of this trend is that a decline in births can also mean a decline in motherhood in general. According to my analysis of data from the Census Bureau, the decline of American motherhood is real, occurring very quickly, and may continue for some time yet. Not only are moms making up less of the population, but their characteristics are changing too, and in a way that might be linked to their proportional decline. Moms today tend to be than in the past. Just looking at recent years, the change in age-specific birth rates has been drastic. In just the past few years, the peak childbearing age range for American women has advanced from that of 25 to 29 to that of 30 to 34. Meanwhile, childbearing among women under 20 has fallen by half or more, while childbearing among women 35 and older is rising. One positive consequence of this age shift is that a larger proportion of new mothers are economically prepared to raise children. Less positively, however, many women find that, as they age, they can't have as many kids as they would like. Plus, having children later in life can increase the risk of health complications. These finer points aside, one major consequence of the older mom's trend is that fewer years of a woman's life are spent as a mother. This means that, at any given time, a larger share of women, and thus of the whole population, will report not having children in government surveys. In other words, later motherhood means less motherhood. Even as motherhood rates decline, Mother's Day, of course, will endure. In fact, despite the demographic shift, retail spending on the holiday appears to be rising. It is hard to say if Mother's Day spending is rising more than one would expect, given that the American population keeps growing. But one factor might be that the proportion of women who are the mothers of adult children is rising, and those adult children may spend more generously when it comes to celebrating the moms they no longer live with. Since NASA published a paper in 1989 claiming that houseplants can soak up pollution and toxic chemicals, businesses and homeowners have increasingly invested in greenery to help clean their air. But a new analysis suggests it could actually take more than 1,000 plants per square meter to gain a benefit any greater than simply opening a couple of windows. The problem lies in the fact that NASA conducted their tests in sealed containers that do not simulate the conditions in most people's homes or offices. The space agency was primarily concerned about keeping the air fresh for astronauts cut off in biospheres or space stations, and helping to combat sick building syndrome, which had become a problem due to the super-insulated and energy-efficient offices of the late 1970s. By the early 1980s, workers regularly complained of skin rashes, sleepiness, headaches, and allergies as they breathed in toxic chemicals from paints and plastics. NASA found that certain plants could remove chemicals from the air, and even today garden centers recommend the plants for air cleaning properties. However, a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years found that houseplants in a normal environment have little impact. In fact, natural ventilation is far better at cleaning the air. The researchers also calculated the clean air delivery rate for plants in the studies they analyzed, and found that the rate at which plants disperse the compounds was well below the usual rate of air exchange in a normal building, caused by the movement of people coming and going, opening doors and windows. Many of the studies did show a reduction in the concentration of volatile organic compounds over time, which is likely why people have seized on them to praise the air-purifying virtues of plants. But the researchers' calculations showed it would take 10 to 1,000 plants per square meter of floor space to compete with the air -cleaning capacity of a building's air handling system, or even just a couple of open windows in a house. In contrast, NASA's SEALED experiment recommended one pot plant per 100 square feet. This is certainly an example of how scientific findings can be misleading or misinterpreted over time, but it's also a great example of how scientific research should continually re-examine and question findings to get closer to the ground truth of understanding what's actually happening.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say he did before buying the blender?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Read numerous comments users put online.`},{key:`B`,text:`Blended all his food without using a machine.`},{key:`C`,text:`Searched for the state-of-the-art models of blenders.`},{key:`D`,text:`Did thorough research on the price of kitchen appliances.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I read through maybe hundreds of online user reviews."。正确选项为 "Read numerous comments users put online."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she has never thought of doing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Eating any blended food.`},{key:`B`,text:`Buying a blender herself.`},{key:`C`,text:`Using machines to do her cooking.`},{key:`D`,text:`Making soups and juices for herself.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I've never thought of getting one myself."。正确选项为 "Buying a blender herself."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say is a really healthy habit?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Cooking every meal creatively in the kitchen.`},{key:`B`,text:`Paying due attention to his personal hygiene.`},{key:`C`,text:`Eating breakfast punctually every morning.`},{key:`D`,text:`Making his own fresh fruit juice regularly.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："I make a fresh fruit juice in the morning."。正确选项为 "Making his own fresh fruit juice regularly."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about store-bought juice from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`One-tenth of it is sugar.`},{key:`B`,text:`It looks healthy and attractive.`},{key:`C`,text:`One's fancy may be tickled by it.`},{key:`D`,text:`It contains an assortment of nutrients.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 1）依据："Did you know that store-bought juice is like 10% sugar?"。正确选项为 "One-tenth of it is sugar."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What is the question the woman asked Mr Grimm after the introduction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`How he has made himself popular as the mayor of Berkton.`},{key:`B`,text:`How the residents will turn Berkton into a tourist attraction.`},{key:`C`,text:`How charming he himself considers the village of Berkton to be.`},{key:`D`,text:`How he has led people of Berkton to change the village radically.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："Thomas Benjamin Grimm, the Mayor of Birkton, is here to talk about his job and responsibilities overseeing this charming village."。正确选项为 "How he has led people of Berkton to change the village radically."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Birkton of ten years ago?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was developed only to a limited extent.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was totally isolated as a sleepy village.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was relatively unknown to the outside.`},{key:`D`,text:`It was endowed with rare natural resources.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："In about ten years, Birkton has gone from a relatively unheard-of, sleepy village to a must-see destination."。正确选项为 "It was relatively unknown to the outside."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What resulted from the passing of the legislative proposal Motion 836?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The people in Berkton were in a harmonious atmosphere.`},{key:`B`,text:`The majority of residents lived in harmony with their neighbors.`},{key:`C`,text:`The majority of residents enjoyed cosy housing conditions.`},{key:`D`,text:`All the houses in Berkton looked aesthetically similar.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："This legislative proposal essentially set out to harmonise the aesthetic appearance of all the houses in Birkton."。正确选项为 "All the houses in Berkton looked aesthetically similar."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the man say the tourists are a blessing to Birkton?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They have helped boost the local economy.`},{key:`B`,text:`They have made the residents unusually proud.`},{key:`C`,text:`They have contributed considerably to its popularity.`},{key:`D`,text:`They have brought happiness to everyone in the village.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Conversation 2）依据："The tourists we receive are a blessing, as they have completely revitalised our local economy."。正确选项为 "They have helped boost the local economy."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about a team of researchers at Northwestern University?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They have created the smallest remote-controlled walking robot in the world.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are going to publish their research findings in the journal Science Robotics.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are the first to build a robot that can bend, crawl, walk, turn and even jump.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are engaged in research on a remote-controlled robot which uses special power.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："In research published in the journal Science Robotics, a team at Northwestern University said the crab -like robot is 0."。正确选项为 "They have created the smallest remote-controlled walking robot in the world."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What did the researchers say about the robot they created?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It changes its shape by complex hardware.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is operated by a special type of tiny motor.`},{key:`C`,text:`It moves from one place to another by memory.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is powered by the elastic property of its body.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："The engineers said this is because the robot is powered by the elastic property of its body."。正确选项为 "It is powered by the elastic property of its body."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What do the researchers expect their robots to do in the future?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Replace humans in exploratory tasks.`},{key:`B`,text:`Perform tasks in tightly confined spaces.`},{key:`C`,text:`Explore the structure of clogged arteries.`},{key:`D`,text:`Assist surgeons in highly complex surgery.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 1）依据："While the research is still in the exploratory phase, the team believes that technology could lead to micro-sized robots that can perform practical tasks in tightly confined spaces."。正确选项为 "Perform tasks in tightly confined spaces."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say she did on her ride to the Isles of Scilly?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She threw up in the bathroom.`},{key:`B`,text:`She slept during the entire ride.`},{key:`C`,text:`She dozed off for a few minutes.`},{key:`D`,text:`She boasted of her marathon race.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "She slept during the entire ride."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about elite sleepers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are mostly immune to cognitive impairment.`},{key:`B`,text:`They can sleep soundly during a rough ride at sea.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are genetically determined to need less sleep.`},{key:`D`,text:`They constitute about 13 percent of the population.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "They are genetically determined to need less sleep."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What has the speaker been trying to find out over the past year?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Whether there is a way to reach elite status.`},{key:`B`,text:`Whether it is possible to modify one's genes.`},{key:`C`,text:`Whether having a baby impacts one's passion.`},{key:`D`,text:`Whether one can train themselves to sleep less.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Whether one can train themselves to sleep less."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What has the speaker discovered after a year of tough scientific study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is in fact quite possible to nurture a passion for sleep.`},{key:`B`,text:`Babies can severely disrupt their parents' sleep patterns.`},{key:`C`,text:`Being forced to rise early differs from being an early bird.`},{key:`D`,text:`New parents are forced to jump out of bed at the crack of dawn.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Being forced to rise early differs from being an early bird."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does a new paper in the Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggest?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`We have poor awareness of how many controversial issues are being debated.`},{key:`B`,text:`No one knows better than yourself what you are thinking about at the moment.`},{key:`C`,text:`No one can change your opinions more than those who speak in a convincing tone.`},{key:`D`,text:`We are likely to underestimate how much we can be swayed by a convincing article.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："And yet, a new paper in the Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggests that we actually have very poor awareness of our own belief change, meaning that we will tend to underestimate how much we've been swayed by a convincing article."。正确选项为 "We are likely to underestimate how much we can be swayed by a convincing article."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What happened when the students read a text that argued against their own initial position?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Their belief about physical punishment changed.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their memory pushed them toward a current belief.`},{key:`C`,text:`The memory of their initial belief came back to them.`},{key:`D`,text:`Their experiences of physical punishment haunted them.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："The students' belief about physical punishment changed when they read a text that argued against their own initial position."。正确选项为 "Their belief about physical punishment changed."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What did the researchers concede concerning their findings?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They apparently have little to do with moderate beliefs.`},{key:`B`,text:`They don't reflect the changes of view on physical punishment.`},{key:`C`,text:`They may not apply to changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are unlikely to alter people's position without more evidence.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。听力原文（Passage 1 (2)）依据："It's likely the findings would be different in the context of changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs."。正确选项为 "They may not apply to changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker conclude from her analysis of the Census Bureau's data?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`American moms have been increasingly inclined to live alone.`},{key:`B`,text:`The American population has been on the rise in the past 25 years.`},{key:`C`,text:`American motherhood has actually been on the decline.`},{key:`D`,text:`The fertility rates in America have in fact been falling sharply.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "American motherhood has actually been on the decline."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say is a positive consequence of the age shift in childbearing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`More new mothers tend to take greater care of their children.`},{key:`B`,text:`More new mothers are economically able to raise children.`},{key:`C`,text:`A larger proportion of women take pride in their children.`},{key:`D`,text:`A larger proportion of women really enjoy motherhood.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "More new mothers are economically able to raise children."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What might be one explanation for the rise in retail spending on Mother's Day?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The meaning of motherhood has changed considerably.`},{key:`B`,text:`More and more mothers go shopping to treat themselves.`},{key:`C`,text:`More mothers have adult children celebrating the holiday.`},{key:`D`,text:`The number of American mothers has been growing steadily.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "More mothers have adult children celebrating the holiday."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does NASA's 1989 paper claim houseplants can do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Add to indoor toxic pollutants.`},{key:`B`,text:`Absorb poisonous chemicals.`},{key:`C`,text:`Beautify the home environment.`},{key:`D`,text:`Soak up surrounding moisture.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："Since NASA published a paper in 1989 claiming that houseplants can soak up pollution and toxic chemicals, businesses and homeowners have increasingly invested in greenery to help clean their air."。正确选项为 "Absorb poisonous chemicals."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What is said to be the problem with NASA's study reported in its 1989 paper?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`NASA did experiments in sealed containers resembling the super-insulated offices of the 1970s.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was based on experiments under conditions unlike those in most homes or offices.`},{key:`C`,text:`NASA conducted tests in outer space whose environment is different from ours.`},{key:`D`,text:`It drew its conclusion without any contrastive data from other experiments.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："The problem lies in the fact that NASA conducted their tests in sealed containers that do not simulate the conditions in most people's homes or offices."。正确选项为 "It was based on experiments under conditions unlike those in most homes or offices."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What is the finding of a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Natural ventilation proves much more efficient for cleaning the air than house plants.`},{key:`B`,text:`House plants disperse chemical compounds more quickly with people moving around.`},{key:`C`,text:`Natural ventilation turns out to be most effective with doors and windows wide open.`},{key:`D`,text:`House plants in a normal environment rarely have any adverse impact on the air.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："However, a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years found that houseplants in a normal environment have little impact."。正确选项为 "Natural ventilation proves much more efficient for cleaning the air than house plants."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does NASA's SEALED experiment recommendation exemplify in scientists' pursuit of truth? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The root cause for misinterpretations of scientific findings.`},{key:`B`,text:`The difficulty in understanding what's actually happening.`},{key:`C`,text:`The steps to be taken in arriving at any conclusion with certainty.`},{key:`D`,text:`The necessity of continually re-examining and challenging findings.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。听力原文（Passage 2）依据："In contrast, NASA's SEALED experiment recommended one pot plant per 100 square feet."。正确选项为 "The necessity of continually re-examining and challenging findings."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`adore`,B:`appraise`,C:`assaults`,D:`chemistry`,E:`coincidence`,F:`cracks`,G:`deported`,H:`dismisses`,I:`illegally`,J:`lost`,K:`perpetually`,L:`prescribed`,M:`shrewd`,N:`skeptical`,O:`strives`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-cp-1`,text:`The Sun Is Also a Star is a truly lovely story of love, romance, fate, and destiny. Natasha is a Jamaican-born immigrant living`}]},n,{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-p1`,title:`Passage One`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-p1-1`,text:`The "American Dream" promises that in the Land of Opportunity, any individual can climb the economic ladder and prosper through hard work and ambition alone. And yet, young Americans today are struggling to earn more than their parents did at the same age, and upward mobility in the US actually compares unfavourably to that of other industrialised nations. So why does the idea of the American Dream persist? A new study in the American Journal of Political Science identifies one factor that has been overlooked: the influence of reality TV. Reality shows have come to dominate US television over the past 20 years, notes Eunji Kim from Vanderbilt University. And the overwhelming majority of these have a "rags-to-riches" storyline: they feature ordinary Americans who work hard to achieve great economic success. And while these programmes are regularly among the most-watched shows, news broadcasts—which paint a more realistic view of the economic hardship faced by millions of Americans—get a much smaller proportion of the viewership. Rags-to-riches stories are ubiquitous on TV—but does watching these programmes actually convince people that economic mobility is easily attainable? To find out, Kim's team had participants watch a 5-minute clip from a reality show with a rags-to-riches storyline. Control participants watched a clip from a reality show that didn't have a rags-to-riches story. After watching the shows, participants rated how much they agreed with four statements relating to the American Dream. The results showed that those who'd watched a rags-to-riches clip did indeed have a significantly greater belief in the American Dream. Interestingly, when participants were separated by party affiliation, this effect was significant among Republicans but not Democrats, suggesting that the kind of messages implicit in these TV shows may play into people's existing socioeconomic beliefs. Kim also conducted a survey of 3,000 US residents. They also rated the extent to which they believed success in life is related to various internal factors (such as ambition) and external factors (such as family wealth). Finally, they read a list of TV programmes and indicated which they regularly watched. Participants who were heavy viewers of rags-to-riches programmes or frequent viewers had a stronger belief in the American Dream than those who never watched such shows. Kim concludes that "rags-to-riches entertainment media are an important cultural force that promotes and perpetuates beliefs in upward mobility". And here's the problem: if people mistakenly believe that hard work is all that is needed for individuals to make a better life for themselves, they may be less supportive of policies that could actually combat inequality. "In this era of choice, entertainment media are what captures hearts and minds," Kim writes. "Its political consequences are anything but trivial".`}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-p2`,title:`Passage Two`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-p2-1`,text:`When someone asks us 'what do you do?' We nearly always reply with our occupation. Work, for many of us, is much more than a job. It is the defining aspect of our identity. For many of us it is through our job that we can define ourselves. "Without my job I don't know who I am," is a sentence that has been uttered on more than a handful of occasions from my office chair. Indeed, it can be one of the most challenging aspects I work on with clients who have lost or been forced into changing their jobs. This loss provokes an identity crisis much greater than the loss of the job itself. One of the things I have come to understand, however, is that our identity is much more complex than we recognise at first glance. If we take the time to reflect we might recognise that as well as our work we can also identify as a friend, a spouse, a son or daughter, a parent, a member of a sports team or religious community. We may recognise that we feel and act differently in these roles and relationships than we do at work. The passive daughter becomes an assertive leader at work. Furthermore, our identities at work are not static. They change over time. I myself have been a shop assistant, a waitress, a student, a graduate, and a clinical psychologist. At each stage my ability to adapt to and develop my career identity has been crucial to my wellbeing. Whilst we like to eliminate uncertainty in our lives at some level we have to manage uncertainty, especially in today's volatile and ever-shifting job market. How we see ourselves is central to the issue of our identity. When we tell ourselves "I'm good at starting projects but not so great at seeing them through" it can become part of our belief system. But if you have the unfortunate experience of an enforced job change you will need to examine those beliefs to see how grounded in reality they are. You will be required to ask yourself how helpful these beliefs are and consider personal change. We can change our beliefs, behaviours and emotional experience at any time through experimentation, practice and conscious self-discipline. In an age where career progression may lead us into new sectors it is ever more important to challenge our sense of self and explore whether you can create a new experience of your identity by changing the beliefs you hold about yourself in order to expand your career options. Ultimately it is you who define who you are. You are only your job if you let it be so.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`I`,explanation:`答案 I（illegally）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`O`,explanation:`答案 O（strives）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`H`,explanation:`答案 H（dismisses）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`E`,explanation:`答案 E（coincidence）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`G`,explanation:`答案 G（deported）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`N`,explanation:`答案 N（skeptical）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（adore）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`F`,explanation:`答案 F（cracks）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`J`,explanation:`答案 J（lost）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`D`,explanation:`答案 D（chemistry）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`There is general consensus among experts that willpower alone cannot guarantee one's success in changing and maintaining a habit.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："The research on whether we have finite or infinite willpower is inconclusive, but experts do generally agree that you can't change and sustain a habit if you rely on your willpower alone."，与题干陈述 "There is general consensus among experts that willpower alone cannot guarantee one's success in changing and maintaining a habit." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`One need not abandon their goals completely just because they missed their target temporarily; they can start anew.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`正确答案 I。I 段："Just because you might have temporarily strayed off course doesn't mean you can't start afresh the next day."，与题干陈述 "One need not abandon their goals completely just because they missed their target temporarily; they can start anew." 信息对应，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Research shows it is quite another challenge to maintain a behavioral change after you have initiated it.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。A 段："According to recent research, behavioral change involves physical changes in the brain."，与题干陈述 "Research shows it is quite another challenge to maintain a behavioral change after you have initiated it." 信息对应，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`It is wrong to assume the strategies we use to start a change of behavior will work equally well in helping maintain it.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段："One of the reasons for this is because we mistakenly believe the strategies we used to initiate the change will be equally effective in helping us continue the change."，与题干陈述 "It is wrong to assume the strategies we use to start a change of behavior will work equally well in helping maintain it." 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Sometimes, it may not be successful to simply substitute one activity with another to effect a change of habit; you may need extra reinforcement.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`正确答案 H。H 段："For example, if you want to stop checking your email first thing in the morning, you might decide to substitute another activity in its place."，与题干陈述 "Sometimes, it may not be successful to simply substitute one activity with another to effect a change of habit; you may need extra reinforcement." 信息对应，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`One should introduce something new to replace an old habit instead of simply kicking it.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`正确答案 F。F 段："When your attention is on doing something new, you give your habit system a chance to operate."，与题干陈述 "One should introduce something new to replace an old habit instead of simply kicking it." 信息对应，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Perfectionists focus too much on their big target and neglect celebrating the small gains they make in the process.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`正确答案 J。J 段："They focus too much on the big goal and don't take the time to celebrate the small progress they make in the process."，与题干陈述 "Perfectionists focus too much on their big target and neglect celebrating the small gains they make in the process." 信息对应，选 J。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`It is of great benefit to us in the long term to conquer the inertia that stops us from making behavioral changes.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`正确答案 L。L 段："Remember, overcoming the behavioral inertia that prevents us from implementing new changes, like eating a healthy diet or exercising, can benefit us in the long run and can improve our physical and mental health."，与题干陈述 "It is of great benefit to us in the long term to conquer the inertia that stops us from making behavioral changes." 信息对应，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`The strategy that successfully changed one of your behaviors may not work for some other behavior of yours.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`正确答案 G。G 段："Because we are creatures of habit, it's natural to assume that when we do manage to adopt and sustain a desirable behavior, that same strategy will work when we want to make another behavior change."，与题干陈述 "The strategy that successfully changed one of your behaviors may not work for some other behavior of yours." 信息对应，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Without a happy mood, it seems that our wisdom to adopt a different behavior vanishes.`,options:e(n.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段："If our mood is low, the wisdom to behave differently seems to disappear and we go back to eating more and exercising less."，与题干陈述 "Without a happy mood, it seems that our wisdom to adopt a different behavior vanishes." 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about young Americans of today?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They have greater ambitions than their parents.`},{key:`B`,text:`They find it difficult to achieve upward mobility.`},{key:`C`,text:`They have overtaken their parents in terms of earnings.`},{key:`D`,text:`They envy the opportunities in other industrialised nations.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："And yet, young Americans today are struggling to earn more than their parents did at the same age, and upward mobility in the US actually compares unfavourably to that of other industrialised nations."。正确选项为 "They find it difficult to achieve upward mobility."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What does Kim's team find about reality TV shows in America?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They reinterpret the essence of the popular rags-to-riches culture.`},{key:`B`,text:`They urge people to achieve economic success through hard work.`},{key:`C`,text:`They help strengthen people's conviction in the American Dream.`},{key:`D`,text:`They feature ordinary Americans striving for social recognition.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage One）："To find out, Kim's team had participants watch a 5-minute clip from a reality show with a rags-to-riches storyline."。正确选项为 "They help strengthen people's conviction in the American Dream."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about news broadcasts in America?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They attract far fewer viewers than reality TV.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are bent on reporting the dark side of life.`},{key:`C`,text:`They stand in striking contrast with reality TV.`},{key:`D`,text:`They focus on Americans' economic hardships.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："And while these programmes are regularly among the most-watched shows, news broadcasts—which paint a more realistic view of the economic hardship faced by millions of Americans—get a much smaller proportion of the viewership."。正确选项为 "They attract far fewer viewers than reality TV."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What can we infer from the passage about Republicans in general?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They believe strongly in the American Dream.`},{key:`B`,text:`They strive to climb the socio-economic ladder.`},{key:`C`,text:`They have a very strong affiliation with their party.`},{key:`D`,text:`They tend to watch more rags-to-riches TV shows.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："The "American Dream" promises that in the Land of Opportunity, any individual can climb the economic ladder and prosper through hard work and ambition alone."。正确选项为 "They believe strongly in the American Dream."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What is stated about people who believe in upward mobility?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are likely to blame the government for their plight.`},{key:`B`,text:`They regard political consequences as anything but trivial.`},{key:`C`,text:`They respect individuals striving to climb the social ladder.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are less likely to approve of policies to fight inequality.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："And here's the problem: if people mistakenly believe that hard work is all that is needed for individuals to make a better life for themselves, they may be less supportive of policies that could actually combat inequality."。正确选项为 "They are less likely to approve of policies to fight inequality."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about one's loss of a job?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It compels them to visit a clinical psychologist.`},{key:`B`,text:`It offers them a chance to play different roles.`},{key:`C`,text:`It renders them puzzled about who they are.`},{key:`D`,text:`It forces them to redefine their life's goals.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "It renders them puzzled about who they are."。请回原文（）核对细节。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What has the author come to understand about our identity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is crucial to our emotional wellbeing.`},{key:`B`,text:`It plays a big role in many facets of life.`},{key:`C`,text:`It reflects our changing status in society.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is more complicated than it appears.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）："One of the things I have come to understand, however, is that our identity is much more complex than we recognise at first glance."。正确选项为 "It is more complicated than it appears."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about our identities at work?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are essential to our self-esteem.`},{key:`B`,text:`They evolve with the passage of time.`},{key:`C`,text:`They overrule all other self-perceptions.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are key to understanding ourselves.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage Two）："If we take the time to reflect we might recognise that as well as our work we can also identify as a friend, a spouse, a son or daughter, a parent, a member of a sports team or religious community."。正确选项为 "They evolve with the passage of time."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What do we have to do in today's ever-changing job market?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Strive to develop our social identity.`},{key:`B`,text:`Prepare for different career paths.`},{key:`C`,text:`Try to be assertive at all times.`},{key:`D`,text:`Learn to manage uncertainty.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）："Whilst we like to eliminate uncertainty in our lives at some level we have to manage uncertainty, especially in today's volatile and ever-shifting job market."。正确选项为 "Learn to manage uncertainty."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What should we do to expand our career options?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Alter our perceptions of ourselves.`},{key:`B`,text:`Compare various job opportunities.`},{key:`C`,text:`Look into newly emerging sectors.`},{key:`D`,text:`Exercise self-discipline consciously.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）："In an age where career progression may lead us into new sectors it is ever more important to challenge our sense of self and explore whether you can create a new experience of your identity by changing the beliefs you hold about yourself in order to expand you…"。正确选项为 "Alter our perceptions of ourselves."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`China is rich in bamboo and is the first country to develop and utilize bamboo resources. Bamboo is widely distributed and diverse in varieties across China. It is highly practical and used in many aspects of production and daily life, such as the making of chopsticks, tables and chairs, and the construction of bridges and houses. The Chinese have a fondness for bamboo; since ancient times, countless literati have used bamboo as a theme to create a rich and colorful array of literature and paintings. The bamboo's stem is straight, symbolizing upright character. Bamboo possesses strong vitality and adaptability, surviving tenaciously no matter how harsh the environment is, thus embodying the spirit of resilience. For thousands of years, bamboo has been regarded as a symbol of the Chinese nation's character.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）中国盛产竹子，是最早开发利用竹资源的国家。 → China is rich in bamboo and is the first country to develop and utilize bamboo resources.`,`2）竹子在中国分布广泛，品种丰富。 → Bamboo is widely distributed and diverse in varieties across China.`,`3）竹子实用性强，用于生产和生活的许多方面，如筷子、桌椅的制作和桥梁、房屋的建造。 → It is highly practical and used in many aspects of production and daily life, such as the making of chopsticks, tables and chairs, and the constructio…`,`4）中国人爱竹，自古以来就有无数文人以竹为主题，创作了绚丽多彩的文学和绘画作品。 → The Chinese have a fondness for bamboo; since ancient times, countless literati have used bamboo as a theme to create a rich and colorful array of lit…`,`5）竹子主干(stem) 笔直，象征正直的品格。 → The bamboo's stem is straight, symbolizing upright character.`,`6）竹子具有强大的生命力和适应能力，无论环境多么恶劣，都能够顽强生存，因而寓意坚韧不拔的精神。 → Bamboo possesses strong vitality and adaptability, surviving tenaciously no matter how harsh the environment is, thus embodying the spirit of resilien…`,`7）几千年来，竹子一直被视为中华民族品格的象征。 → For thousands of years, bamboo has been regarded as a symbol of the Chinese nation's character.`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`中国盛产竹子，是最早开发利用竹资源的国家。竹子在中国分布广泛，品种丰富。竹子实用性强，用于生产和生活的许多方面，如筷子、桌椅的制作和桥梁、房屋的建造。中国人爱竹，自古以来就有无数文人以竹为主题，创作了绚丽多彩的文学和绘画作品。竹子主干(stem) 笔直，象征正直的品格。竹子具有强大的生命力和适应能力，无论环境多么恶劣，都能够顽强生存，因而寓意坚韧不拔的精神。几千年来，竹子一直被视为中华民族品格的象征。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）中国盛产竹子，是最早开发利用竹资源的国家。 → China is rich in bamboo and is the first country to develop and utilize bamboo resources. 2）竹子在中国分布广泛，品种丰富。 → Bamboo is widely distributed and diverse in varieties across China. 3）竹子实用性强，用于生产和生活的许多方面，如筷子、桌椅的制作和桥梁、房屋的建造。 → It is highly practical and used in many aspects of production and daily life, such as the making of chopsticks, tables and chairs, and the constructio… 4）中国人爱竹，自古以来就有无数文人以竹为主题，创作了绚丽多彩的文学和绘画作品。 → The Chinese have a fondness for bamboo; since ancient times, countless literati have used bamboo as a theme to create a rich and colorful array of lit…`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2537,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2024`,`6 月`,`第 2 套`],summary:`2024 年 6 月大学英语六级真题（2 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2024 年 6 月 · 第 2 套 · 站内可作答`},"cet6-2024-06-s3":{parts:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development. In the era of information explosion, the ability to learn independently is essential for anyone who wants to keep pace with the times. Firstly, independent learning allows individuals to acquire knowledge beyond the classroom, which is vital for adapting to the rapidly changing job market. Moreover, it fosters critical thinking and problem-solving skills, enabling individuals to tackle challenges effectively. For instance, self-learners are more likely to explore various resources and devise innovative solutions to problems. A student learning programming can set a weekly goal, compare tutorials, and test an idea in a small project. Keeping a record of mistakes and adjusting the plan helps turn scattered information into usable knowledge. These habits also prepare the student to learn unfamiliar tools after graduation. In conclusion, the significance of independent learning ability cannot be overstated. It is the key to unlocking one's full potential and achieving success in both personal and professional life.`,guidance:[`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development." You ca…。参考范文约 163 词、1 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,`范文结构线索：Firstly / Moreover / In conclusion——这些衔接语标示了论点展开与收束的顺序，可作为自己写作时的段落框架参考。`,`自评建议：对照题目要求逐项检查——是否回应全部提问、段落之间是否有明确逻辑连接、句式与词汇是否匹配话题、词数是否落在规定区间。`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development." You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`题目要求：Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence "Nowadays, cultivating independent learning ability is becoming increasingly crucial for personal development." You ca…。参考范文约 163 词、1 段，为机构自拟参考版本，非官方发布答案，评分以是否切题、结构完整、用词准确为主。`,score:15}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）；本套听力原文暂未收录。`,segments:[]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say he did before buying the blender?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Read numerous comments users put online.`},{key:`B`,text:`Blended all his food without using a machine.`},{key:`C`,text:`Searched for the state-of-the-art models of blenders.`},{key:`D`,text:`Did thorough research on the price of kitchen appliances.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "Read numerous comments users put online."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,stem:`What does the woman say she has never thought of doing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Eating any blended food.`},{key:`B`,text:`Buying a blender herself.`},{key:`C`,text:`Using machines to do her cooking.`},{key:`D`,text:`Making soups and juices for herself.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Buying a blender herself."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,stem:`What does the man say is a really healthy habit?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Cooking every meal creatively in the kitchen.`},{key:`B`,text:`Paying due attention to his personal hygiene.`},{key:`C`,text:`Eating breakfast punctually every morning.`},{key:`D`,text:`Making his own fresh fruit juice regularly.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Making his own fresh fruit juice regularly."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about store-bought juice from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`One-tenth of it is sugar.`},{key:`B`,text:`It looks healthy and attractive.`},{key:`C`,text:`One's fancy may be tickled by it.`},{key:`D`,text:`It contains an assortment of nutrients.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "One-tenth of it is sugar."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,stem:`What is the question the woman asked Mr Grimm after the introduction?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`How he has made himself popular as the mayor of Berkton.`},{key:`B`,text:`How the residents will turn Berkton into a tourist attraction.`},{key:`C`,text:`How charming he himself considers the village of Berkton to be.`},{key:`D`,text:`How he has led people of Berkton to change the village radically.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "How he has led people of Berkton to change the village radically."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn about Birkton of ten years ago?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It was developed only to a limited extent.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was totally isolated as a sleepy village.`},{key:`C`,text:`It was relatively unknown to the outside.`},{key:`D`,text:`It was endowed with rare natural resources.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "It was relatively unknown to the outside."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,stem:`What resulted from the passing of the legislative proposal Motion 836?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The people in Berkton were in a harmonious atmosphere.`},{key:`B`,text:`The majority of residents lived in harmony with their neighbors.`},{key:`C`,text:`The majority of residents enjoyed cosy housing conditions.`},{key:`D`,text:`All the houses in Berkton looked aesthetically similar.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "All the houses in Berkton looked aesthetically similar."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the man say the tourists are a blessing to Birkton?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They have helped boost the local economy.`},{key:`B`,text:`They have made the residents unusually proud.`},{key:`C`,text:`They have contributed considerably to its popularity.`},{key:`D`,text:`They have brought happiness to everyone in the village.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "They have helped boost the local economy."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about a team of researchers at Northwestern University?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They have created the smallest remote-controlled walking robot in the world.`},{key:`B`,text:`They are going to publish their research findings in the journal Science Robotics.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are the first to build a robot that can bend, crawl, walk, turn and even jump.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are engaged in research on a remote-controlled robot which uses special power.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "They have created the smallest remote-controlled walking robot in the world."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,stem:`What did the researchers say about the robot they created?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It changes its shape by complex hardware.`},{key:`B`,text:`It is operated by a special type of tiny motor.`},{key:`C`,text:`It moves from one place to another by memory.`},{key:`D`,text:`It is powered by the elastic property of its body.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "It is powered by the elastic property of its body."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,stem:`What do the researchers expect their robots to do in the future?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Replace humans in exploratory tasks.`},{key:`B`,text:`Perform tasks in tightly confined spaces.`},{key:`C`,text:`Explore the structure of clogged arteries.`},{key:`D`,text:`Assist surgeons in highly complex surgery.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Perform tasks in tightly confined spaces."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say she did on her ride to the Isles of Scilly?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`She threw up in the bathroom.`},{key:`B`,text:`She slept during the entire ride.`},{key:`C`,text:`She dozed off for a few minutes.`},{key:`D`,text:`She boasted of her marathon race.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "She slept during the entire ride."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about elite sleepers?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They are mostly immune to cognitive impairment.`},{key:`B`,text:`They can sleep soundly during a rough ride at sea.`},{key:`C`,text:`They are genetically determined to need less sleep.`},{key:`D`,text:`They constitute about 13 percent of the population.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "They are genetically determined to need less sleep."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,stem:`What has the speaker been trying to find out over the past year?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Whether there is a way to reach elite status.`},{key:`B`,text:`Whether it is possible to modify one's genes.`},{key:`C`,text:`Whether having a baby impacts one's passion.`},{key:`D`,text:`Whether one can train themselves to sleep less.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "Whether one can train themselves to sleep less."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,stem:`What has the speaker discovered after a year of tough scientific study?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It is in fact quite possible to nurture a passion for sleep.`},{key:`B`,text:`Babies can severely disrupt their parents' sleep patterns.`},{key:`C`,text:`Being forced to rise early differs from being an early bird.`},{key:`D`,text:`New parents are forced to jump out of bed at the crack of dawn.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "Being forced to rise early differs from being an early bird."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,stem:`What does a new paper in the Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggest?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`We have poor awareness of how many controversial issues are being debated.`},{key:`B`,text:`No one knows better than yourself what you are thinking about at the moment.`},{key:`C`,text:`No one can change your opinions more than those who speak in a convincing tone.`},{key:`D`,text:`We are likely to underestimate how much we can be swayed by a convincing article.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "We are likely to underestimate how much we can be swayed by a convincing article."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,stem:`What happened when the students read a text that argued against their own initial position?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Their belief about physical punishment changed.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their memory pushed them toward a current belief.`},{key:`C`,text:`The memory of their initial belief came back to them.`},{key:`D`,text:`Their experiences of physical punishment haunted them.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "Their belief about physical punishment changed."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,stem:`What did the researchers concede concerning their findings?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They apparently have little to do with moderate beliefs.`},{key:`B`,text:`They don't reflect the changes of view on physical punishment.`},{key:`C`,text:`They may not apply to changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs.`},{key:`D`,text:`They are unlikely to alter people's position without more evidence.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "They may not apply to changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker conclude from her analysis of the Census Bureau's data?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`American moms have been increasingly inclined to live alone.`},{key:`B`,text:`The American population has been on the rise in the past 25 years.`},{key:`C`,text:`American motherhood has actually been on the decline.`},{key:`D`,text:`The fertility rates in America have in fact been falling sharply.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "American motherhood has actually been on the decline."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,stem:`What does the speaker say is a positive consequence of the age shift in childbearing?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`More new mothers tend to take greater care of their children.`},{key:`B`,text:`More new mothers are economically able to raise children.`},{key:`C`,text:`A larger proportion of women take pride in their children.`},{key:`D`,text:`A larger proportion of women really enjoy motherhood.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "More new mothers are economically able to raise children."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,stem:`What might be one explanation for the rise in retail spending on Mother's Day?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The meaning of motherhood has changed considerably.`},{key:`B`,text:`More and more mothers go shopping to treat themselves.`},{key:`C`,text:`More mothers have adult children celebrating the holiday.`},{key:`D`,text:`The number of American mothers has been growing steadily.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。正确选项为 "More mothers have adult children celebrating the holiday."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,stem:`What does NASA's 1989 paper claim houseplants can do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Add to indoor toxic pollutants.`},{key:`B`,text:`Absorb poisonous chemicals.`},{key:`C`,text:`Beautify the home environment.`},{key:`D`,text:`Soak up surrounding moisture.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "Absorb poisonous chemicals."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,stem:`What is said to be the problem with NASA's study reported in its 1989 paper?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`NASA did experiments in sealed containers resembling the super-insulated offices of the 1970s.`},{key:`B`,text:`It was based on experiments under conditions unlike those in most homes or offices.`},{key:`C`,text:`NASA conducted tests in outer space whose environment is different from ours.`},{key:`D`,text:`It drew its conclusion without any contrastive data from other experiments.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。正确选项为 "It was based on experiments under conditions unlike those in most homes or offices."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,stem:`What is the finding of a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Natural ventilation proves much more efficient for cleaning the air than house plants.`},{key:`B`,text:`House plants disperse chemical compounds more quickly with people moving around.`},{key:`C`,text:`Natural ventilation turns out to be most effective with doors and windows wide open.`},{key:`D`,text:`House plants in a normal environment rarely have any adverse impact on the air.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。正确选项为 "Natural ventilation proves much more efficient for cleaning the air than house plants."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,stem:`What does NASA's SEALED experiment recommendation exemplify in scientists' pursuit of truth? That's the end of Listening Comprehension.`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`The root cause for misinterpretations of scientific findings.`},{key:`B`,text:`The difficulty in understanding what's actually happening.`},{key:`C`,text:`The steps to be taken in arriving at any conclusion with certainty.`},{key:`D`,text:`The necessity of continually re-examining and challenging findings.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。正确选项为 "The necessity of continually re-examining and challenging findings."。（本套听力原文未收录，请结合录音核对。）`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`bounces`,B:`completely`,C:`dispersion`,D:`eccentric`,E:`hanging`,F:`optical`,G:`originates`,H:`perceive`,I:`permeates`,J:`ponder`,K:`preceding`,L:`recklessly`,M:`relation`,N:`spectrum`,O:`stands`},passages:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-cp-1`,text:`A rainbow is a multi-colored, arc-shaped phenomenon that can appear in the sky. The colors of a rainbow are produced by the reflection and Rainbows present a A rainbow is formed when light (generally sunlight) passes through water droplets`}]},r,{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1`,title:`Passage One`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-1`,text:`Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, "Well, that's exactly what I need right now," their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-2`,text:`Most frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-3`,text:`If you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-4`,text:`Despite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word "sarkazein" which literally means "to tear or strip the flesh off." Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word "cutting" and that it hurts.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-5`,text:`What's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-6`,text:`Alternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-7`,text:`Now, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-8`,text:`So, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p1-9`,text:`Thus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.`}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2`,title:`Passage Two`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-1`,text:`Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-2`,text:`This principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category "dog" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-3`,text:`"There are over ten different names for this basic principle," says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. "Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-4`,text:`To identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-5`,text:`The researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. "These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning," says Raviv.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-6`,text:`The impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-7`,text:`But why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-8`,text:`Another theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical (非典型的) examples.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-9`,text:`A third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-10`,text:`"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives," explains Raviv. "For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory."`},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-p2-11`,text:`"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic," concludes Raviv."Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?"`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`C`,explanation:`答案 C（dispersion）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`H`,explanation:`答案 H（perceive）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`M`,explanation:`答案 M（relation）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 M。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`F`,explanation:`答案 F（optical）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`N`,explanation:`答案 N（spectrum）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`O`,explanation:`答案 O（stands）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 O。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`E`,explanation:`答案 E（hanging）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`A`,explanation:`答案 A（bounces）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 A。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`G`,explanation:`答案 G（originates）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 G。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:``,answer:`B`,explanation:`答案 B（completely）。请对照词库与上下文的语意、词性作出判断，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`Although employees are said to be fatigued by meetings, the condition has not been considered worthy of further research until recently.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。D 段："It isn't ground-breaking to say workers feel fatigued after a meeting, but only in recent decades have scientists deemed the condition worthy of further investigation."，与题干陈述 "Although employees are said to be fatigued by meetings, the condition has not been considered worthy of further research until recently." 信息对应，选 D。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`Mroz and his team compiled a list of what to do and what not to do to remedy the problem of MRS.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`正确答案 H。H 段："Drawing from around 200 papers to compile their comprehensive list, Mroz and his team may now hold a remedy to the largely undefined problem of MRS."，与题干陈述 "Mroz and his team compiled a list of what to do and what not to do to remedy the problem of MRS." 信息对应，选 H。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Companies can get rid of the root cause of MRS if they give priority to workers' time.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`正确答案 N。N 段："By reshaping the way they prioritise employees' time, companies can eliminate the very sources of MRS in their tracks."，与题干陈述 "Companies can get rid of the root cause of MRS if they give priority to workers' time." 信息对应，选 N。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`If workers are exhausted to a dangerous degree, it is extremely hard for them to transition to the next task.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`正确答案 F。F 段："As humans, when we transition from one task to another on the job—say from sitting in a meeting to doing normal work—it takes an effortful cognitive switch."，与题干陈述 "If workers are exhausted to a dangerous degree, it is extremely hard for them to transition to the next task." 信息对应，选 F。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Employees in America spend a lot of time attending meetings while the number of hours managers meet is several times more.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。B 段："Every week, employees spend about six hours in meetings, while the average manager meets for a staggering 23 hours."，与题干陈述 "Employees in America spend a lot of time attending meetings while the number of hours managers meet is several times more." 信息对应，选 B。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`Phyllis Hartman has learned by herself many of the ways Mroz suggested in his study and made remarkable success in freeing herself from unnecessary meetings.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`K`,explanation:`正确答案 K。K 段："Despite the relative scarcity of research behind the subject, Hartman has taught herself many of the same tricks suggested in Mroz's study, and has come a long way since her days of being stuck with unnecessary meetings."，与题干陈述 "Phyllis Hartman has learned by herself many of the ways Mroz suggested in his study and made remarkable success in freeing herself from unnecessary me…" 信息对应，选 K。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`When meetings continue too long or don't engage employees, they deplete vitality.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`正确答案 E。E 段："Meetings drain vitality if they last too long, fail to engage employees or turn into one-sided lectures."，与题干陈述 "When meetings continue too long or don't engage employees, they deplete vitality." 信息对应，选 E。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`When the time of meetings is reduced, employees will be more engaged in the meetings they do participate in.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`正确答案 I。I 段："" Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to more employee engagement in the meetings they do attend, which experts agree is a proven remedy for MRS."，与题干陈述 "When the time of meetings is reduced, employees will be more engaged in the meetings they do participate in." 信息对应，选 I。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Some employees consider meetings one of the most dispensable parts of the workday.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。C 段："And though experts agree that traditional meetings are essential for making certain decisions and developing strategy, some employees view them as one of the most unnecessary parts of the workday."，与题干陈述 "Some employees consider meetings one of the most dispensable parts of the workday." 信息对应，选 C。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`According to Mroz, if all his suggestions were applied, a very obvious change would be a steep decrease in the number of meetings scheduled.`,options:e(r.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`正确答案 L。L 段："If an organisation were to apply all 22 suggestions from Mroz and Allen's findings, the most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on the schedule, Mroz says."，与题干陈述 "According to Mroz, if all his suggestions were applied, a very obvious change would be a steep decrease in the number of meetings scheduled." 信息对应，选 L。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the author say sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Both are recognized when heard.`},{key:`B`,text:`Both have exactly the same tone.`},{key:`C`,text:`Both mean the opposite of what they appear to.`},{key:`D`,text:`Both have hidden in them an evident irritation.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage One）："Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them."。正确选项为 "Both are recognized when heard."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`How do many people feel when they hear sarcastic comments?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They feel hostile towards the sarcastic person.`},{key:`B`,text:`They feel belittled and disrespected.`},{key:`C`,text:`They feel a strong urge to retaliate.`},{key:`D`,text:`They feel incapable of disguising their irritation.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："Despite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible."。正确选项为 "They feel belittled and disrespected."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What happens when a person consistently acts sarcastically?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They feel their dignity greatly heightened.`},{key:`B`,text:`They feel increasingly insecure and hostile.`},{key:`C`,text:`They endure hostility under the disguise of humor.`},{key:`D`,text:`They taste bitterness even in pleasant interactions.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage One）："What's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity."。正确选项为 "They feel increasingly insecure and hostile."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say about people quitting sarcastic comments?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`It makes others happier and more self-confident.`},{key:`B`,text:`It restrains them from being irritating and bullying.`},{key:`C`,text:`It benefits not only themselves but also those around them.`},{key:`D`,text:`It shields them from negative comments and outright hostility.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage One）："Despite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible."。正确选项为 "It benefits not only themselves but also those around them."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What is the chief difference between a speaker's wit and sarcasm?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Their clarity.`},{key:`B`,text:`Their appreciation.`},{key:`C`,text:`Their emphasis.`},{key:`D`,text:`Their intention.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage One）："Thus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor."。正确选项为 "Their intention."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about infants learning the category "dog" if they are exposed to Chihuahuas only?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`They will encounter some degree of difficulty.`},{key:`B`,text:`They will try to categorise other objects first.`},{key:`C`,text:`They will prefer Chihuahuas to other dog species.`},{key:`D`,text:`They will imagine Chihuahuas in various conditions.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`正确答案 A。原文依据（Passage Two）："For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category "dog" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs."。正确选项为 "They will encounter some degree of difficulty."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What does Raviv say about the four different kinds of variability?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Which of them is most relevant to the task at hand is to be confirmed.`},{key:`B`,text:`Why they have an impact on learning is far from being understood.`},{key:`C`,text:`Why they have never been directly compared remains a mystery.`},{key:`D`,text:`Which of them is most conducive to learning is yet to be identified.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）：""These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning," says Raviv."。正确选项为 "Which of them is most conducive to learning is yet to be identified."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`How does one of the theories explain the importance of variability for learning new skills?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Learners regard variable training as typical of what happens in the real world.`},{key:`B`,text:`Learners receiving variable training are compelled to reorganise their memories.`},{key:`C`,text:`Learners pay attention to the relevant aspects of a task and ignore those irrelevant.`},{key:`D`,text:`Learners focus on related skills instead of wasting time and effort on unrelated ones.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`正确答案 B。原文依据（Passage Two）："A third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories."。正确选项为 "Learners receiving variable training are compelled to reorganise their memories."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What does the passage say about face recognition?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`People growing up in a small community may find it easy to remember familiar faces.`},{key:`B`,text:`Face recognition has a significant impact on literally every aspect of our social lives.`},{key:`C`,text:`People growing up in a large community can readily recognise any individual faces.`},{key:`D`,text:`The size of the community people grow up in impacts their face recognition ability.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`正确答案 D。原文依据（Passage Two）：""For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community."。正确选项为 "The size of the community people grow up in impacts their face recognition ability."。`,score:1},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does Raviv hope to do with their research work?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`Highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not to learning a skill.`},{key:`B`,text:`Use the principle of variability in teaching seemingly unrelated skills in education.`},{key:`C`,text:`Arouse people's interest in variability and stimulate more research on the topic.`},{key:`D`,text:`Apply the principle of variability to such fields of study as the immune system.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`正确答案 C。原文依据（Passage Two）："" "We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic," concludes Raviv."。正确选项为 "Arouse people's interest in variability and stimulate more research on the topic."。`,score:1}]},{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Fans have been cherished by the Chinese since ancient times, but now they are not just tools for cooling off in the summer; they are more often appreciated as works of art. Many fans have elegant shapes, fine craftsmanship, and are adorned with exquisite patterns of landscapes, flowers, birds, and figures, possessing great artistic value. Numerous renowned Chinese painters and calligraphers enjoy composing poetry and painting on fans to showcase their artistic taste. Fans are often given as gifts to express beautiful wishes and sincere emotions. Today, the practical function of fans has been greatly diminished, but as a cultural symbol and form of art, fans continue to play a significant role in traditional Chinese culture.`,guidance:[`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点：`,`1）扇子自古以来就深受中国人喜爱，但现在已不只是消暑纳凉的工具，而更多地作为艺术品供人欣赏。 → Fans have been cherished by the Chinese since ancient times, but now they are not just tools for cooling off in the summer; they are more often apprec…`,`2）许多扇子造型优美、做工精良，并绘有山水、花鸟、人物等精美图案，具有很高的艺术价值。 → Many fans have elegant shapes, fine craftsmanship, and are adorned with exquisite patterns of landscapes, flowers, birds, and figures, possessing grea…`,`3）中国许多著名画家和书法家喜欢在扇子上作诗绘画，展示其艺术品味。 → Numerous renowned Chinese painters and calligraphers enjoy composing poetry and painting on fans to showcase their artistic taste.`,`4）扇子常作为礼物赠予他人，表达美好的祝福和真挚的情感。 → Fans are often given as gifts to express beautiful wishes and sincere emotions.`,`5）如今，扇子的实用功能已大为减弱，但作为一种文化符号和艺术形式，扇子仍然在中国传统文化中扮演着重要角色。 → Today, the practical function of fans has been greatly diminished, but as a cultural symbol and form of art, fans continue to play a significant role…`]},questions:[{id:`cet6-2024-06-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`扇子自古以来就深受中国人喜爱，但现在已不只是消暑纳凉的工具，而更多地作为艺术品供人欣赏。许多扇子造型优美、做工精良，并绘有山水、花鸟、人物等精美图案，具有很高的艺术价值。中国许多著名画家和书法家喜欢在扇子上作诗绘画，展示其艺术品味。扇子常作为礼物赠予他人，表达美好的祝福和真挚的情感。如今，扇子的实用功能已大为减弱，但作为一种文化符号和艺术形式，扇子仍然在中国传统文化中扮演着重要角色。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`汉译英，参考译文为机构参考版本，非官方标准答案。按中文分句与参考译文逐一对应的要点： 1）扇子自古以来就深受中国人喜爱，但现在已不只是消暑纳凉的工具，而更多地作为艺术品供人欣赏。 → Fans have been cherished by the Chinese since ancient times, but now they are not just tools for cooling off in the summer; they are more often apprec… 2）许多扇子造型优美、做工精良，并绘有山水、花鸟、人物等精美图案，具有很高的艺术价值。 → Many fans have elegant shapes, fine craftsmanship, and are adorned with exquisite patterns of landscapes, flowers, birds, and figures, possessing grea… 3）中国许多著名画家和书法家喜欢在扇子上作诗绘画，展示其艺术品味。 → Numerous renowned Chinese painters and calligraphers enjoy composing poetry and painting on fans to showcase their artistic taste. 4）扇子常作为礼物赠予他人，表达美好的祝福和真挚的情感。 → Fans are often given as gifts to express beautiful wishes and sincere emotions.`,score:15}]}],minutes:125,words:2482,tags:[`真题`,`六级`,`2024`,`6 月`,`第 3 套`],summary:`2024 年 6 月大学英语六级真题（3 套）站内完整版本：写作、听力理解（含逐段原文与站内合成朗读）、阅读理解（选词填空 / 长篇阅读 / 仔细阅读）、翻译全部分区可在线作答，共 57 题，附答案与原文依据解析。`,subtitle:`2024 年 6 月 · 第 3 套 · 站内可作答`}};export{i as default};